atorname{tan}⁡t\\,k$ is \n\n$$\nt\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\\,t\\ne .\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression? You may assume that $n$ is an integer." 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186115516 1074 "To find the domain of $f$, we need the intersection of the domains of the individual components.\n\n- The domain of the 1st component $\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$ is $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\n- The domain of the 2nd component $\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ is $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\n- The domain of the 3rd component $\\operatorname{tan}⁡t$ is $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$, $t\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi ,$ where $n$ is an integer.\n\nTherefore, the domain of the vector-valued function $f(t)$ is \n\n$$\nt\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\\,t\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer." 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186115789 1068 #edb/ref 17592186113671 13194139533446 17592186045585 false] [17592186115789 1068 #edb/ref 17592186363767 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186115789 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186115789 1071 "A supply crate of mass $18\\,\\text{kg}$ is dropped vertically downward with an initial velocity of $2.4\\,\\text{m/s}$ and instantaneous acceleration of $8.2\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$ Let $v(t)$ be the crate's velocity, in meters per second, $t\\ge 0$ seconds after release, with downward taken as the positive direction. The air resistance on the crate is proportional to its velocity and acts in the direction opposite to motion.\n\nFind, rounded to one decimal place, the velocity of the supply crate after $4$ seconds.\nAssume that only gravity, $g=9.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2},$ and drag act on the crate.\n\nThe velocity of the supply crate after $4$ seconds is\n{{field-1}}$\\,\\text{m/s}.$" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186115789 1071 "A supply crate of mass $18kg$ is dropped vertically downward with an initial velocity of $2.4m/s$ and instantaneous acceleration of $8.2m/s^{2}$. Let $v(t)$ be the crate's velocity, in meters per second, $t \\ge 0$ seconds after release, with downward taken as the positive direction. The air resistance on the crate is proportional to its velocity and acts in the direction opposite to motion.\n\nFind, rounded to one decimal place, the velocity of the supply crate after $4$ seconds.\nAssume that only gravity, $g = 9.8m/s^{2}$, and drag act on the crate.\n\nThe velocity of the supply crate after $4$ seconds is\n{{field-1}} $m/s$.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139533446 17592186045585 false] [17592186115789 1074 "First, note that only two forces act on the crate: the force due to gravity, given by the weight\n\n$$\nw=mg=18\\cdot 9.8=176.4\\,\\text{N},\n$$\n\nand the force due to drag $D,$ which is proportional to the velocity (and negative since it acts in the negative direction),\n\n$$\nD=-kv,\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality. Hence, the resultant force acting on the crate is\n\n$$\nF=w+D=176.4-kv.\n$$\n\nApplying Newton's second law, we obtain an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =ma \\\\ 176.4-kv & =18\\,\\frac{dv}{dt} \\\\ \\frac{dv}{dt}+\\frac{k}{18}v & =9.8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that, at a particular moment, the crate’s velocity is $2.4\\,\\text{m/s}$ and its instantaneous acceleration is $8.2\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$ Substituting these values into the ODE, we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n8.2+\\frac{k}{18}\\cdot 2.4=9.8\\,⟹\\,k=12\\,\\text{kg/s}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\frac{dv}{dt}+\\frac{2}{3}v=9.8\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${v}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{v}_{c}}{dt}+\\frac{2}{3}{v}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{v}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-2t/3},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{v}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${v}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{v}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+\\frac{2}{3}\\alpha =9.8\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =14.7\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${v}_{p}(t)=14.7.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nv(t)={v}_{c}(t)+{v}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-2t/3}+14.7.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the crate is released with an initial velocity of $2.4\\,\\text{m/s}.$ So, we can apply the initial condition $v(0)=2.4,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(0) & =A{e}^{-2(0)/3}+14.7 \\\\ 2.4 & =A+14.7 \\\\ A & =-12.3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $v(t):$\n\n$$\nv(t)=-12.3{e}^{-2t/3}+14.7\n$$\n\nFinally, the velocity of the supply crate after $t=4$ seconds is\n\n$$\nv(4)=-12.3{e}^{-2(4)/3}+14.7=13.845…≈13.8\\,\\text{m/s},\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place." 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186115791 1068 #edb/ref 17592186113673 13194139533446 17592186045585 false] [17592186115791 1068 #edb/ref 17592186363769 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186115791 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186115791 1071 "A ball of mass $16\\,\\text{kg}$ falls vertically downward. Let $v$ be its velocity, with downward taken as the positive direction.\nThe air resistance on the ball is proportional to its velocity and acts in the opposite direction to the motion.\nAt a particular moment dur