 that, at a particular moment, the ball’s velocity is $16\\,\\text{m/s}$ and its instantaneous acceleration is $4.9\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$ Substituting these values into the above, we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}156.8-16k & =16\\cdot 4.9 \\\\ 156.8-16k & =78.4 \\\\ -16k & =-78.4 \\\\ k & =4.9\\,\\text{kg/s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the resultant force acting on the ball is\n\n$$\nF=156.8-4.9v.\n$$\n\nWhen the ball reaches terminal velocity ${v}_{\\text{term}},$ the resultant force is zero:\n\n$$\n156.8-4.9{v}_{\\text{term}}=0\\,⟹\\,{v}_{\\text{term}}=\\frac{156.8}{4.9}=32\n$$\n\nTherefore, the terminal velocity of the ball is ${v}_{\\text{term}}=32\\,\\text{m/s}.$" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186119788 1038 #edb/ref 17592186364118 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186119809 1038 #edb/ref 17592186364357 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186119839 1038 #edb/ref 17592186363940 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186119839 1038 #edb/ref 17592186364308 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186119840 1038 #edb/ref 17592186363030 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186119930 1038 #edb/ref 17592186363390 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186119950 1038 #edb/ref 17592186363016 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186119951 1038 #edb/ref 17592186363533 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186120253 1068 #edb/ref 17592186117647 13194139533446 17592186045585 false] [17592186120253 1068 #edb/ref 17592186363758 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186120253 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186120253 1071 "Find an equation of the plane that passes through the point $Q(-2, 2, 0)$ and is perpendicular to the line\n${x = 3 - 4t; y = 1 + 6t; z =-1 + 2t$." 13194139533446 17592186045585 false] [17592186120253 1071 "Find an equation of the plane that passes through the point $Q(-2,2,0)$ and is perpendicular to the line \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=3-4t \\\\ y=1+6t \\\\ z=-1+2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186120253 1074 "Note that the position vector of $Q$ is $q=⟨-2,2,0⟩.$ Converting the parametric equations of the line to vector form, we get\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=3-4t \\\\ y=1+6t \\\\ z=-1+2t\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,⟨x,y,z⟩=⟨3,1,-1⟩+t\\text{ }{{⟨-4,6,2⟩}_{⏟}}_{v}.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe direction vector of the line is $v=⟨-4,6,2⟩,$ so a normal vector to the plane is \n\n$$\nn=v=⟨-4,6,2⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-q)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n-q\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =q\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-4,6,2⟩ & =⟨-2,2,0⟩\\cdot ⟨-4,6,2⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-4,6,2⟩ & =20.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186120306 1068 #edb/ref 17592186117945 13194139533446 17592186045585 false] [17592186120306 1068 #edb/ref 17592186364084 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186120306 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186120306 1071 "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nAssume that ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},$ and ${x}_{3}$ are real numbers. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ has only the trivial solution ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$\n- The vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly dependent.\n- The vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly independent." 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186120306 1071 "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$a_{1} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}, a_{2} = \\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}, a_{3} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$.\n\nAssume that $x_{1}, x_{2}$, and $x_{3}$ are real numbers. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. The equation $x_{1}a_{1} + x_{2}a_{2} + x_{3}a_{3} = 0$ has only the trivial solution $x_{1} = x_{2} = x_{3} = 0$.\n2. The vectors $a_{1}, a_{2}$, and $a_{3}$ are linearly dependent.\n3. The vectors $a_{1}, a_{2}$, and $a_{3}$ are linearly independent." 13194139533446 17592186045585 false] [17592186120306 1074 "We say that three vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},$ and ${v}_{3}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\nThe equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ corresponds to the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ {x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve the system, we create its augmented matrix and reduce it using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we conclude that the basic variables are ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$, while ${x}_{3}$ is the free variable. \n\nSince there is one free variable, the equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ has an infinite number of nonzero solutions, which means that the vectors are linearly dependent. \n\nTherefore, statements I and III are false, while statement II is true." 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186120324 1068 #edb/ref 17592186118053 13194139533446 17592186045585 false] [17592186120324 1068 #edb/ref 17592186364090 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186120324 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186120324 1071 "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nAssume that ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},$ and ${x}_{3}$ are real numbers. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$  has an infinite number of nonzero solutions.\n- The vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly dependent.\n- The vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly independent." 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186120324 1071 "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$a_{1} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}, a_{2} = \\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}, a_{3} = \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}$.\n\nAssume that $x_{1}, x_{2}$, and $x_{3}$ are real numbers. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. The equation $x_{1}a_{1} + x_{2}a_{2} + x_{3}a_{3} = 0$ has an infinite number of nonzero solutions.\n2. The vectors $a_{1}, a_{2}$, and $a_{3}$ are linearly dependent.\n3. The vectors $a_{1}, a_{2}$, and $a_{3}$ are linearly independent." 13194139533446 17592186045585 false] [17592186120324 1074 "We say that three vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},$ and ${v}_{3}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\nThe equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ corresponds to the system \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 7{x}_{2}-3{x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve the system, we create its augmented matrix and reduce it using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7 & -3 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7 & -3 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we conclude that the basic variables are ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$, while ${x}_{3}$ is the free variable. \n\nSince there is one free variable, the equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ has an infinite number of nonzero solutions, which means that the vectors are linearly dependent. \n\nTherefore, statement III is false, while statements I and II are true." 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186124424 1071 "Find a point that satisfies the mean value theorem for $f(x,y) = 9 - x^2 - y^2$ on the line segment $\\overline {AB}$, where the position vectors of $A$ and $B$ are $\\mathbf a = \\langle 0, 0\\rangle$ and $\\mathbf b = \\langle 2, 1\\rangle$, respectively." 13194139533446 17592186045585 false] [17592186124424 1071 "Find a point that satisfies the mean value theorem for $f(x,y) = 9 - x^2 - y^2$ on the line segment $\\overline {AB},$ where the position vectors of $A$ and $B$ are $\\mathbf a = \\langle 0, 0\\rangle$ and $\\mathbf b = \\langle 2, 1\\rangle,$ respectively." 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186124424 1074 "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overline{AB}$, then there exists a point $C \\in \\overline{AB}$ with position vector $\\mathbf c$ such that\n\n$$\nf (\\mathbf b) - f (\\mathbf a) = \\nabla f (\\mathbf c) \\cdot (\\mathbf b - \\mathbf a)\n$$\n\nNotice that the function\n\n$$\nf(x, y) = 9 - x^2 - y^2\n$$\n\nis differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overline{AB}$. So, we can apply the mean value theorem.\n\nSubstituting\n\n$$\n\\mathbf a = \\langle 0, 0\\rangle\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\mathbf b = \\langle 2, 1\\rangle\n$$\n\ninto the above expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(2, 1) - f(0, 0) &= ∇f(c) \\cdot (⟨2, 1⟩ - ⟨0, 0⟩) \\\\\n(9 - 2^{2} - 1^{2}) - (9 - 0^{2} - 0^{2}) &= ∇f(c) \\cdot ⟨2, 1⟩ \\\\\n- 5 &= ∇f(c) \\cdot ⟨2, 1⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $\\nabla f$ and evaluating at\n\n$$\n\\mathbf c = \\langle c_1, c_2\\rangle\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∇f &= ⟨ - 2x,-2y⟩, \\\\\n∇f(c) &= ⟨ - 2c_{1},-2c_{2}⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-5 &= ∇f⟨c⟩ \\cdot ⟨2, 1⟩ \\\\\n- 5 &= ⟨ - 2c_{1},-2c_{2}⟩ \\cdot ⟨2, 1⟩ \\\\\n- 5 &= -4c_{1} - 2c_{2} \\\\\n5 &= 4c_{1} + 2c_{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $C$ lies on $\\overline{AB}$. So, let's first find the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{y - y_{a}}{y_{b} - y_{a}} &= \\frac{x - x_{a}}{x_{b} - x_{a}} \\\\\n\\frac{y - 0}{1 - 0} &= \\frac{x - 0}{2 - 0} \\\\\ny &= \\frac{x}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n\\mathbf c = \\langle c_1,c_2\\rangle\n$$\n\nsatisfies the equation of the line, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nc_{2} &= \\frac{c_{1}}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the equation\n\n$$\n4c_1 + 2c_2 = 5\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4c_{1} + 2(\\frac{c_{1}}{2}) &= 5 \\\\\n4c_{1} + c_{1} &= 5 \\\\\n5c_{1} &= 5 \\\\\nc_{1} &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we find\n\n$$\nc_2 = \\dfrac{c_1}{2} = \\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nSo, the point $C$ that satisfies the mean value theorem is\n\n$$\n\\left(1, \\dfrac{1}{2} \\right)\n$$" 13194139533446 17592186045585 false] [17592186124424 1074 "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overline{AB},$ then there exists a point $C \\in \\overline{AB}$ with position vector $\\mathbf c$ such that\n\n$$\nf (\\mathbf b) − f (\\mathbf a) = \\nabla f (\\mathbf c) · (\\mathbf b − \\mathbf a).\n$$\n\nNotice that the function $f(x, y) = 9 - x^2 - y^2$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overline{AB}.$ So, we can apply the mean value theorem.\n\nSubstituting $\\mathbf a =  \\langle 0, 0\\rangle$ and $\\mathbf b = \\langle 2, 1\\rangle$ into the above expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2,1)-f(0,0) & =∇f(c)\\cdot (⟨2,1⟩-⟨0,0⟩) \\\\ (9-{2}^{2}-{1}^{2})-(9-{0}^{2}-{0}^{2}) & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨2,1⟩ \\\\ -5 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨2,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $\\nabla f$ and evaluating at $\\mathbf c = \\langle c_1, c_2\\rangle,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =⟨-2x,-2y⟩, \\\\ ∇f(c) & =⟨-2{c}_{1},-2{c}_{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5 & =∇f⟨c⟩\\cdot ⟨2,1⟩ \\\\ -5 & =⟨-2{c}_{1},-2{c}_{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨2,1⟩ \\\\ -5 & =-4{c}_{1}-2{c}_{2} \\\\ 5 & =4{c}_{1}+2{c}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $C$ lies on $\\overline{AB}.$ So, let's first find the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y-{y}_{a}}{{y}_{b}-{y}_{a}} & =\\frac{x-{x}_{a}}{{x}_{b}-{x}_{a}} \\\\ \\frac{y-0}{1-0} & =\\frac{x-0}{2-0} \\\\ y & =\\frac{x}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\mathbf c = \\langle c_1,c_2\\rangle$ satisfies the equation of the line, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{2} & =\\frac{{c}_{1}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the equation $4c_1 + 2c_2 = 5,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{c}_{1}+2(\\frac{{c}_{1}}{2}) & =5 \\\\ 4{c}_{1}+{c}_{1} & =5 \\\\ 5{c}_{1} & =5 \\\\ {c}_{1} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we find $c_2 = \\dfrac{c_1}{2} = \\dfrac{1}{2}.$ So, the point $C$ that satisfies the mean value theorem is $\\left(1, \\dfrac{1}{2} \\right).$" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186124425 1071 "Find a point that satisfies the mean value theorem for $f(x,y,z) = x^2 + y - z^2$ on the line segment $\\overline{AB}$, where the position vectors of $A$ and $B$ are $\\mathbf a = \\langle 0,0,1\\rangle$ and $\\mathbf b = \\langle 2,3,0\\rangle$, respectively." 13194139533446 17592186045585 false] [17592186124425 1071 "Find a point that satisfies the mean value theorem for $f(x,y,z) = x^2