rset{―}{AB},$ then there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that\n\n$$\nf(b)-f(a)=∇f(c)·(b-a).\n$$\n\nNotice that the function $f(x,y)=2{x}^{3}-y$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, we can apply the mean value theorem.\n\nSubstituting $a=⟨0,1⟩$ and $b=⟨2,2⟩$ into the above expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2,2)-f(0,1) & =∇f(c)\\cdot (⟨2,2⟩-⟨0,1⟩) \\\\ (2(2)^{3}-2)-(2(0)^{3}-1) & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨2,1⟩ \\\\ 15 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨2,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $∇f$ and evaluating at $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2}⟩,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =⟨6{x}^{2},-1⟩, \\\\ ∇f(c) & =⟨6{c}_{1}^{2},-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}15 & =∇f⟨c⟩\\cdot ⟨2,1⟩ \\\\ 15 & =⟨6{c}_{1}^{2},-1⟩\\cdot ⟨2,1⟩ \\\\ 15 & =12{c}_{1}^{2}-1 \\\\ 16 & =12{c}_{1}^{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}^{2} & =\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ {c}_{1} & =\\pm \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}<0<2,$ it's clear that $-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}$ corresponds to the $x$-coordinate of a point that does not lie on $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ Therefore, we can ignore it. Thus, ${c}_{1}=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}.$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $C$ lies on $\\overset{―}{AB}$ to find ${c}_{2}.$ So, let's first find the equation of the line.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y-{y}_{a}}{{y}_{b}-{y}_{a}} & =\\frac{x-{x}_{a}}{{x}_{b}-{x}_{a}} \\\\ \\frac{y-1}{2-1} & =\\frac{x-0}{2-0} \\\\ y-1 & =\\frac{x}{2} \\\\ y & =\\frac{x}{2}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2}⟩$ satisfies the equation of the line, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{2} & =\\frac{{c}_{1}}{2}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, taking into account that ${c}_{1}=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}},$ we obtain that ${c}_{2}=\\frac{{c}_{1}}{2}+1=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}+1.$ So, the point $C$ that satisfies the mean value theorem is $(\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}},\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}+1).$" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186125071 1068 #edb/ref 17592186122996 13194139533446 17592186045585 false] [17592186125071 1068 #edb/ref 17592186364125 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186125071 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186125071 1071 "Find the global maximum of the function $f(x, y) = xy - x$ on the closed triangular region $D$ with vertices $(0, 0), (1, 0), (0, 2)$." 13194139533446 17592186045585 false] [17592186125071 1071 "Find the global maximum of the function $f(x,y)=xy-x$ on the closed triangular region $D$ with vertices $(0,0),\\,(1,0),\\,(0,2).$" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186125071 1074 "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$\n\nTo find the extreme values, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points of $f$ inside the region. \n\nTo determine the critical points, we solve ${f}^{′}=0.$ Computing the derivative gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}y-1 & x\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe derivative exists at each point inside $D,$ and it equals zero when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}y-1=0 \\\\ x=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}x=0 \\\\ y=1.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us a single point $(0,1).$ Since the point $(0,1)$ does not belong to the interior of $D,$ we do not yet include it in the list of candidates for extrema (it lies on the boundary of $D,$ so we will consider it in the next step).\n\n**Step 2.**  Find the points on the boundary of $D$ that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nTo find the points on the boundary that could give rise to extreme values, we parametrize each part of the boundary:\n\n- The line connecting $(0,0)$ and $(1,0)$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\nr=⟨t,0⟩,\\,t\\in (0,1).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(t,0) \\\\  & =(t)(0)-(t) \\\\  & =-t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the derivative of $F(t)$ is constant. So, $F(t)$ does not have critical points on the open interval $t\\in (0,1).$\n\n- The line connecting $(0,0)$ and $(0,2)$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\nr=⟨0,t⟩,\\,t\\in (0,2).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(0,t) \\\\  & =(0)(t)-(0) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $F(t)$ is constant. So, $F(t)$ does not have local extremum points on the open interval $t\\in (0,2).$\n\n- The line connecting $(1,0)$ and $(0,2)$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =⟨1,0⟩+t(⟨0,2⟩-⟨1,0⟩) \\\\  & =⟨1,0⟩+t⟨-1,2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1-t,2t⟩,\\,t\\in (0,1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(1-t,2t) \\\\  & =(1-t)(2t)-(1-t) \\\\  & =-2{t}^{2}+3t-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the critical points of $F(t)$ on the open interval $t\\in (0,1),$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ (-2{t}^{2}+3t-1)^{′} & =0 \\\\ -4t+3 & =0 \\\\ t & =\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation gives one solution. Evaluating the corresponding values of $x$ and $y$ on the boundary, we obtain one candidate point, $(\\frac{1}{4},\\frac{3}{2}).$\n\nAlso, we have to include the corner points of the region as candidates for extrema. So, in total we obtain four candidates:\n\n$$\n(0,0),\\,(1,0),\\,(0,2),\\,(\\frac{1}{4},\\frac{3}{2})\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values. \n\nEvaluating $f(x,y)$ at each of the points mentioned above, we get the following: \n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,0)$ | $(1,0)$ | $(0,2)$ | $(\\frac{1}{4},\\frac{3}{2})$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $0$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ |\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $-1$ at the point $(1,0),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $\\frac{1}{8}$ at the point $(\\frac{1}{4},\\frac{3}{2}).$" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186125124 1068 #edb/ref 17592186123314 13194139533446 17592186045585 false] [17592186125124 1068 #edb/ref 17592186364138 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186125124 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186125124 1071 "Which of the following statements are true given that $T: R^{8} → R^{8}$ is a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T$, and $T(v_{1}) = T(v_{2})$ for some distinct vectors $v_{1}$ and $v_{2}$?\n\n1. $Ker(T) = {0}$\n2. $T$ is invertible\n3. $rank(T) < 8$" 13194139533446 17592186045585 false] [17592186125124 1071 "Which of the following statements are true given that $T:{R}^{8}\\to {R}^{8}$ is a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T,$ and $T({v}_{1})=T({v}_{2})$ for some distinct vectors  ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}?$ \n\n- $\\text{Ker}(T)={0}$\n- $T$ is invertible\n- $\\text{rank}(T)<8$" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186125124 1074 "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{8}$ into ${R}^{8},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{8} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=8 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n\nSince $T$ is a linear transformation, we have that\n\n$$\nT({v}_{1})=T({v}_{2})\\,⟹\\,T({v}_{1}-{v}_{2})=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since ${v}_{1}-{v}_{2}\\in \\text{Ker}(T)$ and ${v}_{1}\\ne {v}_{2},$ we must have that $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}.$\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0},$ the transformation $T$ and the matrix $T$ are not invertible.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $T$ is not invertible, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 8.$ In turn, this means that $\\text{rank}(T)<8.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III 