34af7.png)" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186134082 1068 #edb/ref 17592186130618 13194139533446 17592186045585 false] [17592186134082 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362743 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186134082 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186134082 1071 "Consider a $7 \\cdot 5$ matrix $A$. If $dim(Col(A)) = 4$, then $dim(Null(A^{T})) =$" 13194139533446 17592186045585 false] [17592186134082 1071 "Consider a $7\\times 5$ matrix $A.$ If $\\text{dim}(\\text{Col}(A))=4,$ then $\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}({A}^{T}))=$" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186134082 1074 "The four fundamental subspaces of a matrix $A$ can be schematically visualized, as shown below.\n\n![](images/88/889b83fadd26b377c4dd34143c9b16541630c04e90de3d9b005e7147ead75ad5.png)\n\nIn our case, we are given that $m=7,$ $n=5,$ and $r=4.$ \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}({A}^{T})) & =m-r=7-4=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186134083 1068 #edb/ref 17592186130624 13194139533446 17592186045585 false] [17592186134083 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362770 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186134083 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186134083 1071 "Which of the following subspaces must be orthogonal to $Null(XY)$ for all compatible matrices $X$ and $Y$?\n\n1. $Null(Y^{T}X^{T})$\n2. $Col(XY)$\n3. $Col(Y^{T}X^{T})$" 13194139533446 17592186045585 false] [17592186134083 1071 "Which of the following subspaces must be orthogonal to $\\text{Null}(XY)$ for all compatible matrices $X$ and $Y?$\n\n- $\\text{Null}({Y}^{T}\\,{X}^{T})$\n- $\\text{Col}(XY)$\n- $\\text{Col}({Y}^{T}\\,{X}^{T})$" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186134083 1074 "Recall that $\\text{Null}(XY)$ contains all vectors that, when pre-multiplied by $XY,$ result in the zero-vector $0.$ \n\nWhen pre-multiplying a vector by $XY,$ we actually multiply each row of $XY$ by that vector. So, $\\text{Null}(XY)$ must be orthogonal to the row space of $XY,$ which is $\\text{Col}((XY)^{T})=\\text{Col}({Y}^{T}\\,{X}^{T}).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"\n\n**Note:** In general, the four fundamental subspaces of a matrix $A$ can be schematically visualized, as shown below.\n\n![](images/de/de22dd5b352e1d5be48f4b6ed3b2a4657ae623c1bd288d3464559758ea334af7.png)" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186134116 1068 #edb/ref 17592186130822 13194139533446 17592186045585 false] [17592186134116 1068 #edb/ref 17592186363154 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186134116 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186134116 1071 "$$\nR\\,=\\,b\n$$\n\nLet $A=QR$  be a QR decomposition of an $m\\times n$ matrix $A$ with linearly independent columns, and the matrix equation above gives the least-squares solution of $Ax=b.$ From left to right, what are the missing parts of the equation?" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186134116 1071 "$RA = Ab$\n\nLet $A = QR$ be a QR decomposition of an $m \\cdot n$ matrix $A$ with linearly independent columns, and the matrix equation above gives the least-squares solution of $Ax = b$. From left to right, what are the missing parts of the equation?" 13194139533446 17592186045585 false] [17592186134116 1074 "The least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the normal equation\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}Ax={A}^{T}b.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $A=QR,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}Ax & ={A}^{T}b \\\\ (QR)^{T}QRx & =(QR)^{T}b \\\\ {R}^{T}({Q}^{T}Q)Rx & ={R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ has orthonormal columns, ${Q}^{T}Q=I,$ and we get\n\n$$\n{R}^{T}Rx={R}^{T}\\,{Q}^{T}b.\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${R}^{T}$ is invertible, pre-multiplying by $({R}^{T})^{-1}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}Rx & =({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b \\\\ R\\,x & ={Q}^{T}\\,b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing parts of the equation are $x$ and ${Q}^{T}.$" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186134332 1068 #edb/ref 17592186132001 13194139533446 17592186045585 false] [17592186134332 1068 #edb/ref 17592186364165 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186134332 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186134332 1071 "$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -5 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{q}_{11} & {q}_{12} \\\\ {q}_{21} & {q}_{22} \\\\ {q}_{31} & {q}_{32}\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{r}_{11} & {r}_{12} \\\\ 0 & {r}_{22}\\end{aligned}]}}.\n$$\n\nConsider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above. Given that the columns ${q}_{1},{q}_{2}$ of $Q$ were obtained from the columns of $A$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, find  the value of ${q}_{12}\\cdot {r}_{12}.$" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186134332 1071 "$\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -5 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}^(⏞)^(A) = \\begin{bmatrix}q_{11} & q 12 \\\\ q_{21} & q_{22} \\\\ q_{31} & q_{32}\\end{bmatrix}^(⏞)^(Q)\\begin{bmatrix}r_{11} & r 12 \\\\ 0 & r_{22}\\end{bmatrix}^(⏞)^(R)$.\n\nConsider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above. Given that the columns $q_{1}, q_{2}$ of $Q$ were obtained from the columns of $A$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, find the value of $q_{12} \\cdot r_{12}$." 13194139533446 17592186045585 false] [17592186134332 1074 "Let ${a}_{1},{a}_{2}$ be the columns of $A.$\n\nFirst, we find an orthonormal basis of $\\text{Col}(A)=\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ using the Gram-Schmidt process.\n\n**Step 1**. We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 2**. We We find the vector ${v}_{2}$ orthogonal to $\\text{Span}{{v}_{1}}$ using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{1}}\\,{a}_{2} \\\\  & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]-(-\\frac{9}{9})[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we normalize ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {q}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute $R:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & ={Q}^{T}\\,A \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{3} & \\frac{2}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -5 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 3\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following QR factorization of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -5 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{3} & -\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & -\\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}3 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 3\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}]}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, ${q}_{12}\\cdot {r}_{12}=(-\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}})\\cdot (-3)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}.$" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186134341 1068 #edb/ref 17592186132055 13194139533446 17592186045585 false] [17592186134341 1068 #edb/ref 17592186364171 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186134341 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186134341 1071 "Given the QR decomposition $A = QR$, find the least-squares solution to $Ax = b$, where\n$Q = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}, R = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}, b = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$." 13194139533446 17592186045585 false] [17592186134341 1071 "Given the QR decomposition $A=QR,$ find the least-squares solution to $Ax=b,$ where\n\n$$\nQ=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,R=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186134341 1074 "The least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}Ax={A}^{T}b\n$$\n\nSubstituting $A=QR,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}Ax & ={A}^{T}b \\\\ (QR)^{T}QRx & =(QR)^{T}b \\\\ {R}^{T}({Q}^{T}Q)Rx & ={R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ is orthogonal, ${Q}^{T}Q=I,$ and we get\n\n$$\n{R}^{T}Rx={R}^{T}\\,{Q}^{T}b.\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${R}^{T}$ is invertible, pre-multiplying by $({R}^{T})^{-1}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}Rx & =({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b \\\\ Rx & ={Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting our matrices, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]}}x & =\\overset{{Q}^{T}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]}} \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}1 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]x & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+7{x}_{2}=5 \\\\ 5{x}_{2}=5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=1.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+7(1) & =5 \\\\ {x}_{1} & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the least-squares solution to $Ax=b$ is $[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186134554 1068 #edb/ref 17592186133332 13194139533446 17592186045585 