6045585 true] [17592186152385 1071 "$u→^(A - 2 I)v→^(A - 2 I)w→^(A - 2 I)0; x→^(A + 3 I)y→^(A + 3 I)0$\nGiven the action of the matrices $A - 2I$ and $A + 3I$ on the nonzero vectors $u, v, w, x$ and $y$ as depicted above, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $w$ is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $λ = 2$ of $A$\n2. $u$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $λ = 2$ of $A$\n3. $y$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $λ =-3$ of $A$" 13194139533446 17592186045585 false] [17592186152385 1074 "A *generalized eigenvector* ${v}_{n}$ of rank $n$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda $ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n{v}_{n}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{n-1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }…\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{n-1},{v}_{n-2},…,{v}_{1}\\ne 0.$ An *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, since \n\n$$\nw\\overset{A-2I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\ni.e. $A-2I$ maps $w$ to $0$ in $1$ step, \nthe vector $w$ is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ of $A.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since\n\n$$\nu\\overset{A-2I\\,}{\\to }v\\overset{A-2I\\,}{\\to }w\\overset{A-2I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\ni.e. $A-2I$ maps $u$ to $0$ in $3$ steps, \nthe vector $u$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3,$ not $2.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since\n\n$$\ny\\overset{A+3I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\ni.e. $A+3I$ maps $w$ to $0$ in $1$ step, \nthe vector $y$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1$ (a.k.a. an eigenvector) corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-3$ of $A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186152387 1068 #edb/ref 17592186148823 13194139533446 17592186045585 false] [17592186152387 1068 #edb/ref 17