, we find the image of $v$ under the action of $A+3I:$\n\n$$\n(A+3I)v=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & -5 \\\\ -4 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]={{[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{w}\\ne 0\n$$\n\n- Since we got a nonzero vector in the last step, we now compute the image of $w$ under the action of $A+3I:$\n\n$$\n(A+3I)w=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & -5 \\\\ -4 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=0\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the following chain of maps:\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{v}\\overset{A+3I\\,}{\\to }{{[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{w}\\overset{A+3I\\,}{\\to }{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{0}\n$$\n\nHere, we see that $A+3I$ maps $v$ to $0$ in $2$ steps.\n\nTherefore:\n\n$\\,$ *$v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-3$ of $A.$*\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\"" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186152397 1068 #edb/ref 17592186148883 13194139533446 17592186045585 false] [17592186152397 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362948 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186152397 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186152397 1071 "${lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{nx/2}=$" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186152397 1071 "What is $lim_(n → ∞)(1 + \\frac{1}{n})^{nx/2}$?" 13194139533446 17592186045585 false] [17592186152397 1074 "Recall the following special limit: \n\n$$\ne={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}\n$$\n\nNow, we rewrite the given limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{nx/2} & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{[{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}]}^{x/2} \\\\  & ={[{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}]}^{x/2} \\\\  &