| $X=1$ | $0.1$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ |\n\nCompute ${f}_{Y|X}(1\\,|\\,0)$ given that the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown in the table above. Round your answer to $2$ decimal places." 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186162322 1074 "The conditional probability mass function ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x)=P(Y=y\\,|\\,X=x).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we're required to compute the conditional probability\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y|X}(1\\,|\\,0)=P(Y=1\\,|\\,X=0).\n$$\n\nBy the multiplication law for conditional probability, we have\n\n$$\nP(Y=1\\,|\\,X=0)=\\frac{P(X=0,Y=1)}{P(X=0)}=\\frac{f(0,1)}{{f}_{X}(0)}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability mass function of $X$ and $Y,$ and ${f}_{X}(x)$ is the marginal distribution of $X.$\n\nRecall that the marginal distribution for $X$ corresponds to the row totals. Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(0) & =f(0,0)+f(0,1)+f(0,2) \\\\  & =0.3+0.2+0.1 \\\\  & =0.6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given table, $f(0,1)=0.2.$ \n\nFinally, substituting  ${f}_{X}(0)=0.6$ and $f(0,1)=0.2$ in our formula above, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y|X}(1\\,|\\,0) & =P(Y=1\\,|\\,X=0) \\\\  & =\\frac{f(0,1)}{{f}_{X}(0)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.2}{0.6} \\\\  & ≈0.33.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186162383 1068 #edb/ref 17592186159470 13194139533446 17592186045585 false] [17592186162383 1068 #edb/ref 17592186363328 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186162383 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186162383 1071 "| $f$ | $Y = 0$ | $Y = 2$ | $Y = 4$ | $Y = 6$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $X = 1$ | $\\frac{1}{18}$ | $\\frac{5}{18}$ | $\\frac{1}{9}$ | $\\frac{2}{9}$ |\n| $X = 2$ | $\\frac{1}{9}$ | $\\frac{1}{18}$ | $0$ | $\\frac{1}{6}$ |\n\nCompute $P(2 \\le Y \\le 6 \\mid X = 2)$ given that the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x, y)$ shown in the table above.\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139533446 17592186045585 false] [17592186162383 1071 "| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=4$ | $Y=6$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $\\frac{1}{18}$ | $\\frac{5}{18}$ | $\\frac{1}{9}$ | $\\frac{2}{9}$ |\n| $X=2$ | $\\frac{1}{9}$ | $\\frac{1}{18}$ | $0$ | $\\frac{1}{6}$ |\n\nCompute $P(2\\le Y\\le 6\\,|\\,X=2)$ given that the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown in the table above." 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186162383 1074 "By the multiplication law for conditional probability, the required conditional probability is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2\\le Y\\le 6\\,|\\,X=2) & =\\frac{P((2\\le Y\\le 6),X=2)}{P(X=2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{P(X=2,(2\\le Y\\le 6))}{P(X=2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{P(X=2,Y=2)+P(X=2,Y=4)+P(X=2,Y=6)}{P(X=2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{f(2,2)+f(2,4)+f(2,6)}{{f}_{X}(2)},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability mass function of $X$ and $Y,$ and ${f}_{X}(x)$ is the marginal distribution of $X.$\n\nRecall that the marginal distribution for $X$ corresponds to the row totals. Thus,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(2) & =f(2,0)+f(2,2)+f(2,4)+f(2,6) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}+\\frac{1}{18}+0+\\frac{1}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given table, we have that $f(2,2)=\\frac{1}{18},f(2,4)=0$ and $f(2,6)=\\frac{1}{6}.$ \n\nFinally, substituting our values into our formula above, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(2\\le Y\\le 6\\,|\\,X=2) & =\\frac{f(2,2)+f(2,4)+f(2,6)}{{f}_{X}(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{18}+0+\\frac{1}{6})}{(\\frac{1}{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{2}{9})}{(\\frac{1}{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186166864 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362913 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186166864 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362920 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186166864 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362968 131941395