ce $r={e}^{2\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=2{e}^{2\\theta }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we evaluate our line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{({e}^{2\\theta })}^{2}+{(2{e}^{2\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{e}^{4\\theta }+4{e}^{4\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{5{e}^{4\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{e}^{2\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}{e}^{2\\theta }{|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}({e}^{\\pi }-{e}^{0}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}({e}^{\\pi }-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186172391 1068 #edb/ref 17592186169179 13194139533446 17592186045585 false] [17592186172391 1068 #edb/ref 17592186363520 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186172391 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186172391 1071 "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the maximum value of the function $f(x, y, z) = x + 2y - 6z$ on the intersection of the plane $2x - 3y + 2z = 0$ and the cylinder $x^{2} + y^{2} = 2$." 13194139533446 17592186045585 false] [17592186172391 1071 "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the maximum value of the function $f(x,y,z)=x+2y-6z$ on the intersection of the plane $2x-3y+2z=0$ and the cylinder ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=2.$" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186172391 1074 "We need to find the maximum value of \n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=x+2y-6z\n$$\n\nsubject to the constraints \n\n$$\ng(x,y,z)=2x-3y+2z=0,\\,h(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=2.\n$$\n\nSince $f(x,y,z)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y,z)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ g(x,y,z)=0 \\\\ h(x,y,z)=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe start by expanding the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x},{f}_{y},{f}_{z}⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x},{g}_{y},{g}_{z}⟩+\\mu ⟨{h}_{x},{h}_{y},{h}_{z}⟩ \\\\ ⟨1,2,-6⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2,-3,2⟩+\\mu ⟨2x,2y,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}1=2\\lambda +2\\mu x \\\\ 2=-3\\lambda +2\\mu y \\\\ -6=2\\lambda \\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}x=\\frac{7}{2\\mu } \\\\ y=-\\frac{7}{2\\mu } \\\\ \\lambda =-3\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\mu $ can't be zero since this leads to a contradiction.\n\nNext, we substitute our expressions for $x$ and $y$ into the second constraint:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =2 \\\\ \\frac{49}{4{\\mu }^{2}}+\\frac{49}{4{\\mu }^{2}} & =2 \\\\ \\frac{49}{2{\\mu }^{2}} & =2 \\\\ {\\mu }^{2} & =\\frac{49}{4} \\\\ \\mu  & =\\pm \\frac{7}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\nx=\\pm 1,\\,y=∓1.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x$ and $y$ into the first constraint:\n\n- If $x=1$ and $y=-1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-3y+2z & =0 \\\\ 2(1)-3(-1)+2z & =0 \\\\ 5+2z & =0 \\\\ z & =-\\frac{5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $x=-1$ and $y=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-3y+2z & =0 \\\\ 2(-1)-3(1)+2z & =0 \\\\ -5+2z & =0 \\\\ z & =\\frac{5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(1,-1,-\\frac{5}{2}),\\,(-1,1,\\frac{5}{2}).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y,z)$ | $(1,-1,-\\frac{5}{2})$ | $(-1,1,\\frac{5}{2})$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y,z)$ | $14$ | $-14$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at $(1,-1,-\\frac{5}{2}),$ and its maximum value is $14.$" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186175801 1071 "Suppose that a matrix $A$ has characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda) = (\\lambda-4)^6(\\lambda-9)^8$, the rank of $(A-9I)^3$ equals $9$, and the rank of $(A-9I)^4$ equals $7$. If a basis of $\\mathbb{R}^{14}$ consists of linearly independent generalized eigenvectors of $A$, how many of these are generalized eigenvectors of rank $4$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda = 9$?" 13194139533446 17592186045585 false] [17592186175801 1071 "Suppose that a matrix $A$ has characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda) = (\\lambda-4)^6(\\lambda-9)^8,$ the rank of $(A-9I)^3$ equals $9,$ and the rank of $(A-9I)^4$ equals $7.$ If a basis of $\\mathbb{R}^{14}$ consists of linearly independent generalized eigenvectors of $A$, how many of these are generalized eigenvectors of rank $4$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda = 9?$" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186175801 1074 "For the given matrix $A$ with eigenvalue\n\n$$\n\\lambda = 9\n$$\n\na generalized eigenvector of rank $4$ is a vector $\\mathbf{v}$ such that\n\n$$\n(A - 9I)^4 \\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0} \\quad \\textrm{and} \\quad (A - 9I)^3 \\mathbf{v} \\neq \\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nNotice that $A$ must be a $14 \\times 14$ matrix.\n\n- Since the rank of $(A-9I)^3$ equals $9$, then by the rank-nullity theorem, we have that\n$\\text{nullity}(A-9I)^3 = 14-9 = 5$.\nSo, the solutions to $(A - 9I)^3 \\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0}$ form a subspace $\\text{Null}(A-9I)^3$ of dimension $5$ in $\\mathbb{R}^{14}$.\n- Since the rank of $(A-9I)^4$ equals $7$, then by the rank-nullity theorem, we have that\n$\\text{nullity}(A-9I)^4 = 14-7 = 7$.\nSo, the solutions to $(A - 9I)^4 \\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0}$ form a subspace $\\text{Null}(A-9I)^4$ of dimension $7$ in $\\mathbb{R}^{14}$.\n\nSince $\\text{Null}(A-9I)^3$ is a subspace of dimension $5$ in $\\text{Null}(A-9I)^4$, which is a subspace of dimension $7$, our basis can contain only $2$ vectors that lie in $\\text{Null}(A-9I)^4$ but not in $\\text{Null}(A-9I)^3$.\n\nTherefore, there will be\n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(A-9I)^4 - \\text{nullity}(A-9I)^3 = 7-5=2\n$$\n\ngeneralized eigenvectors of rank $4$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=9$." 13194139533446 17592186045585 false] [17592186175801 1074 "For the given matrix $A$ with eigenvalue $\\lambda = 9,$ a generalized eigenvector of rank $4$ is a vector $\\mathbf{v}$ such that \n\n$$\n(A - 9I)^4 \\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0}\n\\quad \\textrm{and} \\quad\n(A - 9I)^3 \\mathbf{v} \\neq \\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\nNotice that $A$ must be a $14 \\times 14$ matrix.\n\n- Since the rank of $(A-9I)^3$ equals $9,$ then by the rank-nullity theorem, we have that\n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(A-9I)^3 = 14-9 = 5.\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions to $(A - 9I)^3 \\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0}$ form a subspace $\\text{Null}(A-9I)^3$ of dimension $5$ in $\\mathbb{R}^{14}.$\n\n- Since the rank of $(A-9I)^4$ equals $7,$  then by the rank-nullity theorem, we have that\n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(A-9I)^4 = 14-7 = 7.\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions to $(A - 9I)^4 \\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0}$ form a subspace $\\text{Null}(A-9I)^4$ of dimension $7$ in $\\mathbb{R}^{14}.$\n\nSince $\\text{Null}(A-9I)^3$ is a subspace of dimension $5$ in $\\text{Null}(A-9I)^4$, which is a subspace of dimension $7,$ our basis can contain only $2$ vectors that lie in $\\text{Null}(A-9I)^4$ but not in $\\text{Null}(A-9I)^3.$\n\nTherefore, there will be \n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(A-9I)^4 - \\text{nullity}(A-9I)^3 = 7-5=2\n$$\n\n generalized eigenvectors of rank $4$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=9.$" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186175841 1071 "Suppose that a matrix $A$ has characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda) = (1+\\lambda) (4-\\lambda)^3(\\lambda-1)^5$, and for the eigenvalue $\\lambda = 1$, there are exactly $3$ eigenvectors. If a basis of $\\mathbb{R}^{9}$ consists of linearly independent generalized eigenvectors of $A$, how many of these generalized eigenvectors of rank greater than $1$ correspond to the eigenvalue $\\lambda 