 $\\text{Null}(A+I)^{5}$, which is a subspace of dimension $7,$ our basis can contain only $3$ vectors that lie in $\\text{Null}(A+I)^{4}$ but does not lie in $\\text{Null}(A+I)^{5}.$\n\nTherefore, there will be \n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(A+I)^{5}-\\text{nullity}(A+I)^{4}=7-4=3\n$$\n\n generalized eigenvectors of rank $5$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1.$" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186176258 1068 #edb/ref 17592186173776 13194139533446 17592186045585 false] [17592186176258 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362804 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186176258 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186176258 1071 "Suppose that a matrix $B$ has characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda )=(\\lambda +\\sqrt{2})^{3}(\\lambda -2)^{21},$ the rank of $(B-2I)^{6}$ equals $4,$ and the rank of $(B-2I)^{5}$ equals $7.$ If a basis of ${R}^{24}$ consists of linearly independent generalized eigenvectors of $B$, how many of these are generalized eigenvectors of rank $6$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2?$" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186176258 1071 "Suppose that a matrix $B$ has characteristic polynomial $p(λ) = (λ + \\sqrt{2})^{3}(λ - 2)^{21}$, the rank of $(B - 2I)^{6}$ equals $4$, and the rank of $(B - 2I)^{5}$ equals $7$. If a basis of $R^{24}$ consists of linearly independent generalized eigenvectors of $B$, how many of these are generalized eigenvectors of rank $6$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $λ = 2$?" 13194139533446 17592186045585 false] [17592186176258 1074 "For the given matrix $B$ with eigenvalue $\\lambda =2,$ a generalized eigenvector of rank $6$ is a vector $v$ such that \n\n$$\n(B-2I)^{6}v=0\\,\\text{and}\\,(B-2I)^{5}v\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nNotice that $B$ must be a $24\\times 24$ matrix.\n\n- Since the rank of $(B-2I)^{5}$ equals $7,$ then by the rank-nullity theorem, we have that\n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(B-2I)^{5}=24-7=17.\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions to $(B-2I)^{5}v=0$ form a subspace $\\text{Null}(B-2I)