nd $ϕ$ can vary arbitrarily in their respective domains. \n\nTherefore, since $\\rho \\ge 0,$ our surface is a half-plane.\n\n![](images/98/985e43b7773f9e02bf681bacf14839d5963db2678654570e3902a5fcf3b6494f.png)" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186176403 1068 #edb/ref 17592186174646 13194139533446 17592186045585 false] [17592186176403 1068 #edb/ref 17592186363431 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186176403 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186176403 1071 "![](images/bc/bc0e25e290235560cca0c2c0f18b8b22a8f1909e29c1d8706fff6cfc3ca8955a.png)\n\nThe solid enclosed by the quarter-sphere $x^{2} + y^{2} + z^{2} = 9$ for $y \\ge 0$ and $x \\le 0$, shown above, can be described in spherical polar coordinates as" 13194139533446 17592186045585 false] [17592186176403 1071 "![](images/bc/bc0e25e290235560cca0c2c0f18b8b22a8f1909e29c1d8706fff6cfc3ca8955a.png)\n\nThe solid enclosed by the quarter-sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=9$ for $y\\ge 0$ and $x\\le 0,$ shown above, can be described in spherical polar coordinates as" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186176403 1074 "We write the position of a point (vector) in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ),$ where \n\n- $\\rho \\ge 0$ is the distance from the point to the origin,\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ is the angle between the point and the positive $x$-axis, and\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi ]$ is the angle the corresponding vector makes with the positive $z$-axis.\n\n![](images/e3/e33bd1b452217ea524c15bc6fc9555c6e12cd07e929e8aa473ce6e3db1322f0e.png)\n\n  \n\nNotice that inside our region, the spherical polar coordinates vary withing the following domains:\n\n- $\\rho \\in [0,3]$\n\n- $\\theta \\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ]$\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi ]$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 3,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi }.\n$$" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186176404 1068 #edb/ref 17592186174652 13194139533446 17592186045585 false] [17592186176404 1068 #edb/ref 17592186363437 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186176404 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186176404 1071 "![](images/41/41b5ff62b86d0cc7ce341a013f1815b7bb203f5b93cb52c525844f9bb93839b2.png)\n\nThe solid enclosed by the sphere $x^{2} + y^{2} + z^{2} = 4y$, shown above, can be described in spherical polar coordinates as" 13194139533446 17592186045585 false] [17592186176404 1071 "![](images/41/41b5ff62b86d0cc7ce341a013f1815b7bb203f5b93cb52c525844f9bb93839b2.png)\n\nThe solid enclosed by the sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=4y,$ shown above, can be described in spherical polar coordinates as" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186176404 1074 "We write the position of a point (vector) in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ),$ where \n\n- $\\rho \\ge 0$ is the distance from the point to the origin,\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ is the angle between the point and the positive $x$-axis, and\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi ]$ is the angle the corresponding vector makes with the positive $z$-axis.\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $R$ represents the sphere of radius $2$ centered at $(0,2,0).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=4y$ in spherical polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =4y \\\\ {\\rho }^{2} & =4\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ \\\\ \\rho (\\rho -4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $\\rho $ cannot be identical to zero on the entire sphere, we must have $\\rho =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ.$\n\n![](images/3a/3a6985012edbaf28ca42d88fd47e3769fa53704cad8f8c09021c944d966a3d48.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that inside our region, the spherical polar coordinates vary within the following domains:\n\n- $\\rho \\in [0,4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ]$\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,\\pi ]$\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi ]$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi }.\n$$" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186179733 1038 #edb/ref 17592186364378 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186179733 1038 #edb/ref 17592186364391 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186180282 1068 #edb/ref 17592186179206 13194139533446 17592186045585 false] [17592186180282 1068 #edb/ref 17592186364385 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186180282 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186180282 1071 "Let $R = [2, 5] \\cdot [0, 2]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x, y) = 30 - x^{2} - y^{2}$.\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R$, where\n\n$P_{1} = {x_{0}, x_{1}, …, x_{m}}, P_{2} = {y_{0}, y_{1}, …, y_{n}}, P = P_{1} \\cdot P_{2}$.\n\nGiven that the upper Riemann sum of $f$ with respect to $P$ is given by\n$U(f, P) = ∑_(i = 1)^(m)∑_(j = 1)^(n)AAAAAΔx_{i}Δy_{j}$\nwhere $Δx_{i} = x_{i} - x_{i - 1}$ and $Δy_{j} = y_{j} - y_{j - 1}$, what is the missing expression in the double summation above?" 13194139533446 17592186045585 false] [17592186180282 1071 "Let $R=[2,5]\\times [0,2]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)=30-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}.$\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}={{x}_{0},{x}_{1},…,{x}_{m}},\\,{P}_{2}={{y}_{0},{y}_{1},…,{y}_{n}},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that the upper Riemann sum of $f$ with respect to $P$ is given by\n\n$$\nU(f,P)={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}\\boxed{}\\,Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}\n$$\n\nwhere $Δ{x}_{i}={x}_{i}-{x}_{i-1}$ and $Δ{y}_{j}={y}_{j}-{y}_{j-1},$ what is the missing expression in the double summation above?" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186180282 1074 "The upper Riemann sum is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}{M}_{ij}Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${M}_{ij}$ is the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ \n\nWe consider an arbitrary subregion ${R}_{ij}$ of the partition. This subregion lies between ${x}_{i-1}$ and ${x}_{i}$ over the $x$-axis and ${y}_{i-1}$ and ${y}_{i}$ over the $y$-axis, as shown below. \n\n![](images/88/88ffe30ae5401e00502b35d851b216768aad69bf94236561617adf23e1ce058f.png)\n\nNotice that:\n\n- $f(x,y)=30-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ decreases as $x$ increases on $[2,5],$ and\n\n- $f(x,y)=30-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ decreases as $y$ increases on $[0,2].$\n\n![](images/ce/ce0d90127e1f14654f4b32a933f1eb6e76e054c03f19ca40c3f04116aa3c2c93.png)\n\nAs a result, the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *smallest* possible $x$ and the *smallest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *bottom left corner* of each subregion. \n\n![](images/5b/5b9967c814e46914e0409854de0960e85339adc9b9fd9693677c8e048c342702.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{ij} & =f({x}_{i-1},{y}_{j-1})=30-{x}_{i-1}^{2}-{y}_{j-1}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor all $1\\le i\\le m$ and $1\\le j\\le n.$\n\nFinally, the upper Riemann sum is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}(30-{x}_{i-1}^{2}-{y}_{j-1}^{2})Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the missing expression is $30-{x}_{i-1}^{2}-{y}_{j-1}^{2}.$" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186198372 1038 #edb/ref 17592186363471 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186198373 1038 #edb/ref 17592186364654 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186198411 1038 #edb/ref 17592186363478 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186228925 1071 "A supply crate of mass $24\\,\\textrm{kg}$ falls vertically downward. Let $v(t)$ be the crate's velocity, in meters per second, $t \\geq 0$ seconds after the descent began, with downward taken as the positive direction.\nThe air resistance on the crate is proportional to the square of its velocity with a constant of proportionality $k=\\dfrac{4}{5}\\,\\textrm{kg/m}$ and acts in the direction opposite to motion.\n\nWhat differential equation best models the velocity? Assume the acceleration due to gravity is $g$, and the only forces acting on the crate are the weight due to gravity and drag." 13194139533446 17592186045585 false] [17592186228925 1071 "A supply crate of mass $24\\,\\textrm{kg}$ falls vertically downward. Let $v(t)$ be the crate's velocity, in meters per second, $t \\geq 0$ seconds after the descent began, with downward taken as the positive direction.\nThe air resistance on the crate is proportional to the square of its velocity with a constant of proportionality $k=\\dfrac{4}{5}\\,\\textrm{kg/m}$ and acts in the direction opposite to motion.\n\nWhat differential equation best models the velocity? Assume the acceleration due to gravity is $g,$ and the only forces acting on the crate are the weight due to gravity and drag." 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186228925 1074 "Recall Newton's second law, which states that the resultant force $F$ acting on a body equals the product of its mass $m$ and acceleration $a{:}$\n\n$$\nF = ma\n$$\n\nIn our case, two forces are acting on the crate:\n\n- The force due to gravity, given by the weight $w$ of the crate, which equals\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}w & =mg \\\\  & =24g.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The force due to drag (air resistance) $D,$ which is proportional to the square of the velocity of the crate (and negative since it acts in the negative direction):\n\n$$\nD = -kv^2 = -\\dfrac{4}{5}v^2\n$$\n\nSumming the forces, the resultant force acting on the crate is\n\n$$\nF = w + D = 24g - \\dfrac{4}{5}v^2.\n$$\n\nAlso, recall that acceleration is the derivative of velocity: $a=\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}v}{\\mathrm{d}t}.$\nSubstituting this into Newton's second law, along with our expression for the resultant force on the crate, we have\n\n$$\n24g - \\dfrac{4}{5}v^2 =24\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}v}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}v}{\\mathrm{d}t} = g - \\dfrac{1}{30}v^2.\n$$" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186228925 1074 "Recall Newton's second law, which states that the resultant force $F$ acting on a body equals the product of its mass $m$ and acceleration $a{:}$\n$F = ma$\n\nIn our case, two forces are acting on the crate:\n\n- The force due to gravity, given by the weight $w$ of the crate, which equals\n$w|= mg; = 24g$.\n- The force due to drag (air resistance) $D$, which is proportional to the square of the velocity of the crate (and negative since it acts in the negative direction):\n$D = -kv^2 = -\\dfrac{4}{5}v^2$\n\nSumming the forces, the resultant force acting on the crate is\n\n$$\nF = w + D = 24g - \\dfrac{4}{5}v^2\n$$\n\nAlso, recall that acceleration is the derivative of velocity:\n\n$$\na=\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}v}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into Newton's second law, along with our expression for the resultant force on the crate, we have\n\n$$\n24g - \\dfrac{4}{5}v^2 =24\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}v}{\\mathrm{d}t} \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}v}{\\mathrm{d}t} = g - \\dfrac{1}{30}v^2\n$$" 13194139533446 17592186045585 false] [17592186228926 1071 "A concrete cylinder of mass $50\\,\\textrm{kg}$ is released vertically downward with an initial velocity of $9\\,\\textrm{m/s}$. Let $v(t)$ be the cylinder's velocity, in meters per second, $t \\geq 0$ seconds after release, with downward taken as the positive direction.\nThe air resistance on the cylinder is proportional to the square of its velocity with a constant of proportionality $k=4.9\\,\\textrm{kg/m}$ and acts in the direction opposite to motion.\n\nFind the equation for the velocity $v(t)$. Assume the acceleration due to gravity is $g=9.8\\,\\textrm{m/s}^2$, and the only forces acting on the cylinder are the weight due to gravity and drag." 13194139533446 17592186045585 false] [17592186228926 1071 "A concrete cylinder of mass $50\\,\\textrm{kg}$ is released vertically downward with an initial velocity of $9\\,\\textrm{m/s}.$ Let $v(t)$ be the cylinder's velocity, in meters per second, $t \\geq 0$ seconds after release, with downward taken as the positive direction.\nThe air resistance on the cylinder is proportional to the square of its velocity with a constant of proportionality $k=4.9\\,\\textrm{kg/m}$ and acts in the direction opposite to motion.\n\nFind the equation for the velocity $v(t).$ Assume the acceleration due to gravity is $g=9.8\\,\\textrm{m/s}^2,$ and the only forces acting on the cylinder are the weight due to gravity and drag." 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186228926 1074 "First, note that only two forces act on the package: the force due to gravity, given by the weight\n\n$$\nw = mg = 50g\n$$\n\nand the force due to drag $D$, which is proportional to the velocity squared (and negative since it acts in the negative direction):\n\n$$\nD = -kv^2 = -4.9v^2\n$$\n\nHence, the resultant force acting on the package is\n\n$$\nF = w + D =50g - 4.9v^2\n$$\n\nApplying Newton's second law, we obtain a separable first-order differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF &= ma \\\\\n50g - 4.9v^{2} &= 50\\frac{dv}{dt} \\\\\n\\frac{dv}{dt} &= g - 0.098v^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dv}{dt} &= g(1 - 0.01v^{2}) \\\\\n\\frac{1}{1 - 0.01v^{2}} \\cdot \\frac{dv}{dt} &= g \\\\\n\\int\\frac{1}{1 - 0.01v^{2}} \\cdot \\frac{dv}{dt}dt &= g\\intdt \\\\\n\\int(1)/(1 - (0.1v)^{2})dv &= g\\intdt \\\\\n\\frac{1}{0.1}artanh(0.1v) &= gt + C_{1} \\\\\nartanh(0.1v) &= \\frac{1}{10}gt + C \\\\\n0.1v &= \\tanh (\\frac{1}{10}gt + C) \\\\\nv(t) &= 10\\tanh (\\frac{1}{10}gt + C)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C = 0.1C_1$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the package is released with an initial velocity of $9\\,\\textrm{m/s}$. So, we can apply the initial condition $v(0) = 9$, and solve for $C{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv(0) &= 10\\tanh (\\frac{1}{10}g \\cdot 0 + C) \\\\\n9 &= 10\\tanh (C) \\\\\n\\tanh (C) &= 0.9 \\\\\nC &= artanh(0.9) \\approx 1.472\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $v(t){:}$\n\n$$\nv(t) = 10\\tanh\\left(\\dfrac1{10}g t+1.472\\right)\n$$" 13194139533446 17592186045585 false] [17592186228926 1074 "First, note that only two forces act on the package: the force due to gravity, given by the weight\n\n$$\nw = mg = 50g\n$$\n\nand the force due to drag $D,$ which is proportional to the velocity squared (and negative since it acts in the negative direction):\n\n$$\nD = -kv^2 = -4.9v^2\n$$\n\nHence, the resultant force acting on the package is\n\n$$\nF = w + D =50g - 4.9v^2.\n$$\n\nApplying Newton's second law, we obtain a separable first-order differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =ma \\\\ 50g-4.9{v}^{2} & =50\\,\\frac{dv}{dt} \\\\ \\frac{dv}{dt} & =g-0.098{v}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dv}{dt} & =g(1-0.01{v}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{1}{1-0.01{v}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{dv}{dt} & =g \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{1-0.01{v}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{dv}{dt}\\,dt & =g\\int dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{1-(0.1v)^{2}}\\,dv & =g\\int dt \\\\ \\frac{1}{0.1}\\text{artanh}(0.1v) & =gt+{C}_{1} \\\\ \\text{artanh}(0.1v) & =\\frac{1}{10}gt+C \\\\ 0.1v & =\\operatorname{tanh}⁡(\\frac{1}{10}gt+C) \\\\ v(t) & =10\\operatorname{tanh}⁡(\\frac{1}{10}gt+C),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C = 0.1C_1$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the package is released with an initial velocity of $9\\,\\textrm{m/s}.$ So, we can apply the initial condition $v(0) = 9,$ and solve for $C{:}$\n\n