2186362709 1072 #uuid "4952e6c8-6c33-5b76-932a-88fabacd27b6" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186362709 1074 "For the given matrix $C$ with eigenvalue $\\lambda =-6,$ a generalized eigenvector of rank $4$ is a vector $v$ such that \n\n$$\n(C+6I)^{4}v=0\\,\\text{and}\\,(C+6I)^{3}v\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nNotice the following:\n\n- We are given that $\\text{Null}(C+6I)^{3}$ has dimension $2.$ This means that the solutions to $(C+6I)^{3}v=0$ form a subspace of dimension $2.$\n\n- We are given that $\\text{Null}(C+6I)^{4}$ has dimension $6.$ This means that the solutions to $(C+6I)^{4}v=0$ form a subspace of dimension $6.$\n\nNow, since $\\text{Null}(C+6I)^{3}$ is a subspace of dimension $2$ in $\\text{Null}(C+6I)^{4}$, which is a subspace of dimension $6,$ our basis can contain only $4$ vectors that lie in $\\text{Null}(C+6I)^{4}$ but does not lie in $\\text{Null}(C+6I)^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, there will be \n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(C+6I)^{4}-\\text{nullity}(C+6I)^{3}=6-2=4\n$$\n\n generalized eigenvectors of rank $4$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-6.$" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186362710 1075 #edb/ref 17592186362711 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186362710 1075 #edb/ref 17592186362712 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186362710 1075 #edb/ref 17592186362713 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186362710 1075 #edb/ref 17592186362714 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186362710 1075 #edb/ref 17592186362715 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186362710 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186362710 1077 "selection" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186362710 1079 #edb/ref 17592186362715 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186362710 1080 #uuid "f0070d7b-e6f1-5c2a-b236-0cd78dc7b53b" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186362711 1081 "3" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186362711 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186362711 1084 #uuid "89f12690-3a63-551c-b510-20f96310bef2" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186362712 1081 "2" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186362712 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186362712 1084 #uuid "64df770c-14e6-51b7-b5e2-6609c8157928" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186362713 1081 "6" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186362713 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186362713 1084 #uuid "085b8a3b-48a3-5558-81c6-03cca47694ff" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186362714 1081 "1" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186362714 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186362714 1084 #uuid "ffb5aedb-535f-5de6-97b4-1fe57d43401d" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186362715 1081 "4" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186362715 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186362715 1084 #uuid "929cd437-ce88-5c2a-a8a9-5a59a7eab4c4" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186362716 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362717 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186362716 1069 "q-109774" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186362716 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186362716 1071 "Which of the following subspaces must be orthogonal to $\\text{Null}({A}^{T})$ for every matrix $A?$\n\n- $\\text{Col}(A)$\n- $\\text{Null}(A)$\n- $\\text{Col}({A}^{T})$" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186362716 1072 #uuid "7b12af04-ad27-575e-b0d1-aaf05c9b69e1" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186362716 1074 "Recall that $\\text{Null}({A}^{T})$ is the left null space of $A.$ This consists of vectors that, when pre-multiplied by ${A}^{T},$ result in the zero-vector $0.$\n\nWhen pre-multiplying a vector by ${A}^{T},$ we actually multiply each row of ${A}^{T}$, that is, each column of $A$, by that vector. So, $\\text{Null}({A}^{T})$ must be orthogonal to the column space of $A,$ which is $\\text{Col}(A).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"\n\n**Note:** In general, the four fundamental subspaces of a matrix $A$ can be schematically visualized, as shown below.\n\n![](images/de/de22dd5b352e1d5be48f4b6ed3b2a4657ae623c1bd288d3464559758ea334af7.png)" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186362717 1075 #edb/ref 17592186362718 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186362717 1075 #edb/ref 17592186362719 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186362717 1075 #edb/ref 17592186362720 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186362717 1075 #edb/ref 17592186362721 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186362717 1075 #edb/ref 17592186362722 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186362717 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186362717 1077 "selection" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186362717 1079 #edb/ref 17592186362719 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186362717 1080 #uuid "fe489986-82fe-5320-9907-156feba7cc97" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186362718 1081 "I and III only" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186362718 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186362718 1084 #uuid "501cfa61-1bae-5493-9927-44e080eedcb6" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186362719 1081 "I only" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186362719 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186362719 1084 #uuid "e79a9ced-86df-5aba-ba5d-8b38b1a30f4e" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186362720 1081 "III only" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186362720 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186362720 1084 #uuid "d78089f5-2aad-5e43-b5a0-cc7b67658993" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186362721 1081 "I and II only" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186362721 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186362721 1084 #uuid "1fbd0e7e-1a3f-539f-916d-f095722f68be" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186362722 1081 "II only" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186362722 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186362722 1084 #uuid "149e462a-2fa3-515b-a582-18f8ee2ca359" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186362723 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362724 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186362723 1069 "q-109781" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186362723 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186362723 1071 "Which of the following subspaces must be orthogonal to $\\text{Col}({B}^{T})$ for every matrix $B?$\n\n- $\\text{Col}(B)$\n- $\\text{Null}({B}^{T})$\n- $\\text{Null}(B)$" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186362723 1072 #uuid "90aa20cc-7bd4-5b9c-96fe-e3f55743e36c" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186362723 1074 "Recall that $\\text{Col}({B}^{T})$ is the row space of $B.$ This consists of all linear combinations of the rows of $B.$\n\nIf a vector is orthogonal to every linear combination of the rows of $B,$ it must be orthogonal to each row of $B.$\nBut notice that when pre-multiplying a vector by $B,$ we actually multiply each row of $B$ by that vector.\nSo, $\\text{Col}({B}^{T})$ must be orthogonal to the null space of $B,$ which is $\\text{Null}(B).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"\n\n**Note:** In general, the four fundamental subspaces of a matrix $A$ can be schematically visualized, as shown below.\n\n![](images/18/18713ed3d103ca0a02d74cede1e6448012e0aa28a2df804d41cfb277589a0158.png)" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186362724 1075 #edb/ref 17592186362725 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186362724 1075 #edb/ref 17592186362726 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186362724 1075 #edb/ref 17592186362727 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186362724 1075 #edb/ref 17592186362728 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [175