Use the tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ to estimate the value of $f(1).$" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186362893 1072 #uuid "7f9dc4af-4f4e-5627-b6ea-707abe485f5e" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186362893 1074 "The tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at the point $a=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =f(\\frac{\\pi }{4})+{f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that $f(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=0,$ and since ${f}^{′}(x)=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{sec}⁡x,$ then ${f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=2.$ So, the tangent line approximation is given by\n\n$$\nL(x)=2(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4}).\n$$\n\nNow, we can approximate $f(1)$ by evaluating the tangent line approximation at $x=1.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1) & ≈L(1) \\\\  & =2(1-\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =2-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186362894 1075 #edb/ref 17592186362895 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186362894 1075 #edb/ref 17592186362896 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186362894 1075 #edb/ref 17592186362897 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186362894 1075 #edb/ref 17592186362898 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186362894 1075 #edb/ref 17592186362899 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186362894 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186362894 1077 "selection" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186362894 1079 #edb/ref 17592186362899 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186362894 1080 #uuid "0a37bc31-d8c2-5721-a783-ab38279822c0" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186362895 1081 "2-\\frac{\\pi }{4}" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186362895 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186362895 1084 #uuid "be70cf20-a712-5709-b35b-937dc2c