 "q-120155" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186362975 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186362975 1071 "![](images/ae/aeddd324493a5ea0a3650fe2ceea577fbc7d45e1e2758912041f7a4ccb06ec09.png)\n\nThe diagram shown above is the slope field for which of the following differential equations?" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186362975 1072 #uuid "0e0c0ffa-48f6-5f1a-a4a0-2dcd27aa1fff" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186362975 1074 "![](images/62/620d47558e5ae5d054971311b7ec127a3318aaaf8ab10e773abde7bcd4f79a3c.png)\n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $y=-2$ and $y=0.$ Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should equal zero on both of these lines.\n\nOf the given equations, the only equations that satisfy this condition are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =-y(y+2) \\\\ {y}^{′} & =y(y+2)(y-1) \\\\ {y}^{′} & ={y}^{2}(y+2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can dismiss the equation ${y}^{′}=y(y+2)(y-1).$ Although the right-hand side evaluates to zero at $y=-2$ and $y=0,$ it also evaluates to zero when $y=2,$ which is behavior not exhibited by our slope field.\n\n- Second, the lines $y=-2$ and $y=0$ divide the plane into $3$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $3$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| Region | $y<-2$ | $-2<y<0$ | $y>0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $↘$ | $↗$ | $↘$ |\n| Sign | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n\nOf the remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-y(y+2).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${y}^{′}=-y(y+2).$" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186362976 1075 #edb/ref 17592186362977 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186362976 1075 #edb/ref 17592186362978 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [