592186045585 true] [17592186362982 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186362982 1071 "![](images/da/da8d6b73685dbd2db82ad6bdaa528f316fb7819ba3f99f226f0ad9127d3a5609.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- ${y}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{y}$\n- ${y}^{′}=\\frac{2}{{y}^{2}}$\n- ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{y+1}$" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186362982 1072 #uuid "1bc4fffb-9fca-5d90-ae58-a105216385f3" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186362982 1074 "![](images/9b/9b6c8c113f59cc006f26e336b85abde6337a3af2a9f5d6dccc001eee636991ac.png)\n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- The slopes of the slope field become vertical as $y\\to 0.$\n\n- The slopes of the slope field become horizontal as $y\\to \\pm \\infty .$\n\n- The slopes of the slope field are positive for $y<0$ and negative for $y>0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{y}$ gives the required behavior. Notice that ${y}^{′}\\to 0$ as $y\\to \\pm \\infty ,$ and ${y}^{′}\\to \\infty $ as $y\\to 0.$ Also, the right-hand side of this equation is positive for $y<0$ and negative for $y>0.$\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{2}{{y}^{2}}$ does *not* give the required behavior. The slopes of the slope field for this differential equation are positive for $y>0.$\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{y+1}$ does *not* give the required behavior. Notice that\n\n$$\n\\frac{y}{y+1}\\to 0\\,\\text{as}\\,y\\to 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slopes of the slope field for this differential equation do not become vertical as $y\\to 0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186362983 1075 #edb/ref 17592186362984 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186362983 1075 #edb/ref 17592186362985 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186362983 1075 #edb/ref 17592186362986 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186362983 107