6{c}_{3}+3⟩\\cdot ⟨1,-1,-1⟩ \\\\ 0 & =-{c}_{2}+{c}_{1}-3{{c}_{3}}^{2}+6{c}_{3}-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $C$ lies on $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, let's first find the equation of the line.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-0}{1} & =\\frac{y-0}{(-1)}=\\frac{z-1}{(-1)} \\\\ x & =-y=1-z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nParametrizing the line, we get\n\n$$\nx=t\\,\\,y=-t,\\,z=1-t,\\,0\\le t\\le 1.\n$$\n\nSince $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}⟩$ satisfies the equation of the line, we have\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}=t\\,\\,{c}_{2}=-t,\\,{c}_{3}=1-t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above in the equation $-{c}_{2}+{c}_{1}-3{{c}_{3}}^{2}+6{c}_{3}-3=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-(-t)+t-3(1-t)^{2}+6(1-t)-3 & =0 \\\\ t+t-3+6t-3{t}^{2}+6-6t-3 & =0 \\\\ -3{t}^{2}+2t & =0 \\\\ -t(3t-2) & =0 \\\\ t & =0,\\,\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we proceed to find the coordinates of the points that satisfy the mean value theorem.\n\n- For $t=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} & =0, \\\\ {c}_{2} & =-0=0, \\\\ {c}_{3} & =1-0=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the coordinates of the first point $C$ are $(0,0,1).$\n\n- For $t=\\frac{2}{3},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} & =\\frac{2}{3}, \\\\ {c}_{2} & =-\\frac{2}{3}, \\\\ {c}_{3} & =1-\\frac{2}{3}=\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the coordinates of the second point $C$ are $(\\frac{2}{3},-\\frac{2}{3},\\frac{1}{3}).$" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363080 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363081 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363080 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363082 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363080 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363083 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363080 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363084 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363080 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363085 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363080 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363080 1077 "selection" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363080 1079 #edb/ref 175921