585 true] [17592186363199 1081 "$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363199 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363199 1084 #uuid "fb40d380-5dc7-5e14-9da4-4417962eb0dc" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363200 1081 "$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363200 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363200 1084 #uuid "d8830562-3dcc-5248-a8aa-c5cdee386516" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363201 1081 "$[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363201 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363201 1084 #uuid "608033c7-43b2-5dcf-9f24-fcebddc5bc0a" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363202 1068 #edb/ref 17592186363203 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363202 1069 "q-140841" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363202 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363202 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}7 & -1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 7 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 10\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 12\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. \nGiven that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP,$ where $P$ is an orthogonal matrix, which of the following could be the first and second columns of the matrix $P?$" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363202 1072 #uuid "bf5de7bd-3234-5d41-a9a5-c12fb9a085ef" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363202 1074 "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nFirst, note that $A$ is symmetric and $D$ is diagonal. Therefore, $D={P}^{T}\\,AP$ gives an orthogonal diagonalization of $A.$\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=6,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=6,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=12.$ So, the first and second columns of $P$ correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}.$ \n\nNow, we need to find eigenvectors that correspond to ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=6.$ Computing $A-6I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-6I & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & -1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 7 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 10\\end{aligned}]-6[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-6I)x=0$ gives two eigenvectors: \n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\n\nNotice that eigenvectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are not orthogonal. So, we proceed using the Gram-Schmidt process. We set \n\n$$\n{e}_{1}={v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand we find ${e}_{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}_{2} & ={v}_{2}-\\frac{{v}_{2}\\cdot {e}_{1}}{{e}_{1}\\cdot {e}_{1}}{e}_{1} \\\\  & ={v}_{2}+\\frac{2}{5}{e}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{2}{5}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ 1 \\\\ \\frac{2}{5}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, dividing ${e}_{1}$ and ${e}_{2}$ by their respective norms, we obtain the first and second columns of $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{e}_{1}}{‖{e}_{1}‖} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ \\frac{{e}_{2}}{‖{e}_{2}‖} & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{\\sqrt{30}}{5})}[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ 1 \\\\ \\frac{2}{5}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{30}}{30} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{30}}{6} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{30}}{15}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363203 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363204 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363203 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363205 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363203 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363206 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363203 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363207 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363203 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363208 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363203 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363203 1077 "selection" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363203 1079 #edb/ref 17592186363204 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363203 1080 #uuid "43da5e18-1a97-5649-82f0-f3809dd899d2" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363204 1081 "$[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{30}}{30} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{30}}{6} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{30}}{15}\\end{aligned}]$" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363204 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363204 1084 #uuid "02d479be-872e-5b2b-9a6f-55bc9a392083" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363205 1081 "$[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{-2\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363205 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363205 1084 #uuid "8ef4f44e-6d79-526b-a93e-f229a1ed3dfd" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363206 1081 "$[\\begin{ali