92186363465 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363464 1069 "q-162663" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363464 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363464 1071 "Find the domain of the vector-valued function $f(s)=\\sqrt{1-s}\\,i+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(s+2)\\,j+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(-s)\\,k.$" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363464 1072 #uuid "7f5ab3ef-ae4b-58ea-a096-4f661af5c41f" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363464 1074 "To find the domain of $f,$ we need the intersection of the domains of the individual components.\n\n- The domain of $\\sqrt{1-s}$ is $s\\in (-\\infty ,1].$\n\n- The domain of $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(s+2)$ is $s\\in (-2,\\infty ).$\n\n- The domain of $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(-s)$ is $s\\in (-\\infty ,0).$\n\nThe intersection is illustrated in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/6a/6a2d170c22a544335735e859dd080d74964a4d5b5053789a557b5ea406f9d3a9.png)\n\nTherefore, the domain of the vector-valued function $f(s)$ is $s\\in (-2,0).$" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363465 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363466 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363465 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363467 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363465 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363468 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363465 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363469 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363465 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363470 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363465 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363465 1077 "selection" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363465 1079 #edb/ref 17592186363469 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363465 1080 #uuid "6e9a1fab-e156-581f-bef9-19ac8957daa2" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363466 1081 "s\\in (-2,0)\\cup (0,\\infty )" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363466 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363466 1084 #uuid "4b740762-045e-5820-a736-1fe5651b59de" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363467 1081 "s\\in (-2,1]" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363467 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363467 1084 #uuid "a0f05f0a-6614-5d5d-9df5-da7e78375081" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363468 1081 "s\\in (-2,\\infty )" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363468 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363468 1084 #uuid "43be1e1f-d373-53b1-afef-1ef4c39e04b0" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363469 1081 "s\\in (-2,0)" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363469 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363469 1084 #uuid "aef37efd-8619-5880-9e33-8cc7399e16db" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363470 1081 "s\\in (-\\infty ,-2)\\cup (-2,1]" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363470 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363470 1084 #uuid "3bcc0512-6302-5ee0-b419-66a663e9083e" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363471 1068 #edb/ref 17592186363472 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363471 1069 "q-162797" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363471 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363471 1071 "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the maximum value of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+x+y$ given that ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 3.$" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363471 1072 #uuid "0965ed17-df89-5de8-b0cd-60d291248cdf" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363471 1074 "First, we need to find the critical values of the function in the region's interior. We compute the critical points by solving $∇f=0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨2x+1,2y+1⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above equation has the solution $(x,y)=(-\\frac{1}{2},-\\frac{1}{2}).$ This point belongs to our region.\n\nNext, we need to find the maximum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=3.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global maximum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=3\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨2x+1,2y+1⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda =1$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda \\ne 1,$ we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}2x+1=2\\lambda x \\\\ 2y+1=2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}x=\\frac{1}{2(\\lambda -1)} \\\\ y=\\frac{1}{2(\\lambda -1)}\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,x=y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $y=x$ in the condition $g(x,y)=3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =3 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{x}^{2} & =3 \\\\ 2{x}^{2} & =3 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ x & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(-\\frac{1}{2},-\\frac{1}{2}),\\,(-\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2},-\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2}),\\,(\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2}).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(-\\frac{1}{2},-\\frac{1}{2})$ | $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2},-\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2})$ | $(\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2})$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $3-\\sqrt{6}$ | $3+\\sqrt{6}$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at $(\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2}),$ and its maximum value is $3+\\sqrt{6}.$" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363472 1075