ing equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ (3+2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{′} & =0 \\\\ 2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation gives two solutions $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\in (0,2\\pi ).$\nWe also have to check the endpoint of the interval $t=0.$ Therefore, the only values of $t$ that can possibly give rise to extreme values are $t=0,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$ Evaluating the corresponding values of $x$ and $y$ on the boundary, we obtain three candidate points:\n\n$$\n(\\sqrt{3},0),\\,(0,-\\sqrt{3}),\\,(0,\\sqrt{3})\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values.\n\nLet's compare the values of $f(x,y)$ evaluated at each of the points mentioned above. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\sqrt{3},0) & =(\\sqrt{3})^{2}+{0}^{2}+2(0) \\\\  & =3+0+0 \\\\  & =3 \\\\ f(0,-\\sqrt{3}) & =(0)^{2}+(-\\sqrt{3})^{2}+2(-\\sqrt{3}) \\\\  & =0+3-2\\sqrt{3} \\\\  & =3-2\\sqrt{3} \\\\ f(0,\\sqrt{3}) & ={0}^{2}+(\\sqrt{3})^{2}+2(\\sqrt{3}) \\\\  & =0+3+2\\sqrt{3} \\\\  & =3+2\\sqrt{3} \\\\ f(0,-1) & ={0}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+2(-1) \\\\  & =0+1-2 \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $-1$ at the point $(0,-1),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $3+2\\sqrt{3}$ at the point $(0,\\sqrt{3}).$" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363548 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363549 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363548 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363550 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363548 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363551 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363548 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363552 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363548 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363553 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363548 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363548 1077 "selection" 13194139533446 175