n $f(x,y)={y}^{2}+{x}^{2}-2x+1$ on the closed region $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\le 0,\\,y\\ge 0,\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 9},$ shown above." 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363554 1072 #uuid "d4185743-769b-5614-aaad-cb81a0a772dd" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363554 1074 "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$\n\nTo find the extreme values, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points of $f$ inside the region. \n\nTo determine the critical points, we solve  ${f}^{′}=0.$ Computing the derivative gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}2x-2,\\,2y\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe derivative exists at each point inside $D,$ and it equals zero when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}2x-2=0 \\\\ 2y=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}x=1 \\\\ y=0.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us a single point $(1,0).$ Since the point $(1,0)$ does not belong to the interior of $D,$ we do not include it in the list of candidates for extrema.\n\n**Step 2.**  Find the points on the boundary of $D$ that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nTo find the points on the boundary that could give rise to extreme values, we parametrize each part of the boundary:\n\n- The line connecting $(-3,0)$ and $(0,0)$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\nr=⟨t,0⟩,\\,t\\in (-3,0).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on this boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(t,0) \\\\  & ={0}^{2}+{t}^{2}-2t+1 \\\\  & ={t}^{2}-2t+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the critical points of $F(t)$ on the open interval $t\\in (-3,0),$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ ({t}^{2}-2t+1)^{′} & =0 \\\\ 2t-2 & =0 \\\\ t & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution, $t=1$ is not inside the interval $t\\in (-3,0).$\n\n- The line connecting $(0,0)$ and $(0,3)$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\nr=⟨0,t⟩,\\,t\\in (0,3).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on this boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(0,t) \\\\  & ={t}^{2}+{0}^{2}-2(0)+1 \\\\  & ={t}^{2}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the critical points of $F(t)$ on the open interval $t\\in (0,3),$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ ({t}^{2}+1)^{′} & =0 \\\\ 2t & =0 \\\\ t & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution, $t=0$ is not inside the interval $t\\in (0,3).$\n\n- The quarter-circle connecting $(-3,0)$ and $(0,3)$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =⟨t,\\sqrt{9-{t}^{2}}⟩,\\,t\\in (-3,0).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on this boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(t,\\sqrt{9-{t}^{2}}) \\\\  & ={(\\sqrt{9-{t}^{2}})}^{2}+{t}^{2}-2t+1 \\\\  & =9-{t}^{2}+{t}^{2}-2t+1 \\\\  & =10-2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the derivative of $F(t)$ is constant. So, $F(t)$ does not have critical points on the open interval $t\\in (-3,0).$\n\nAlso, we have to include the corner points of the region as candidates for extrema. So, we obtain three candidates on the boundary:\n\n$$\n(-3,0),\\,(0,0),\\,(0,3)\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values. \n\nEvaluating $f(x,y)$ at each of the points mentioned above, we get the following: \n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(-3,0)$ | $(0,0)$ | $(0,3)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $16$ | $1$ | $10$ |\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $1$ at the point $(0,0),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $16$ at the point $(-3,0).$" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363555 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363556 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363555 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363557 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363555 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363558 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363555 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363559 13194139533446 1759218