 ⋯ & 0 & f(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFirst, note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{({J}_{2}(2)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{1}(1))} & ={e}^{([\\begin{aligned}{J}_{2}(2) & 0 \\\\ 0 & {J}_{1}(1)\\end{aligned}])} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{({J}_{2}(2))} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {e}^{({J}_{1}(1))}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={e}^{({J}_{2}(2))}\\,⊕\\,{e}^{({J}_{1}(1))}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we compute the function of each Jordan block separately.\n\n- For ${J}_{2}(2),$ we have $n=2$ and $\\lambda =2.$ since $n=2,$ we need to compute the first derivative of $f$ only:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={e}^{x} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & ={e}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f({J}_{2}(2)) & =[\\begin{aligned}f(2) & {f}^{′}(2) \\\\ 0 & f(2)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2} & {e}^{2} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For ${J}_{1}(1),$ we have $n=1$ and $\\lambda =1.$ \\since $n=1,$ we don't need any derivatives. So, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f({J}_{1}(1)) & =[\\begin{aligned}f(1)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{(1)}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}e\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{({J}_{2}(1)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{2}(2))} & ={e}^{({J}_{2}(1))}\\,⊕\\,{e}^{({J}_{2}(2))} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2} & {e}^{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {e}^{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & e\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363661 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363662 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363661 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363661 1077 "field-3" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363661 1079 #edb/ref 17592186363662 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363661 1080 #uuid "83740fd6-0ede-55a0-90dc-e8e21cddb2fa" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363662 1081 "0" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363662 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [1759