[\\begin{aligned}f(\\lambda ) & {f}^{′}(\\lambda ) & \\frac{{f}^{″}(\\lambda )}{2!} & ⋯ & \\frac{{f}^{(n-1)}(\\lambda )}{(n-1)!} \\\\ 0 & f(\\lambda ) & {f}^{′}(\\lambda ) & ⋯ & \\frac{{f}^{(n-2)}(\\lambda )}{(n-2)!} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋱ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ 0 & ⋯ & 0 & f(\\lambda ) & {f}^{′}(\\lambda ) \\\\ 0 & ⋯ & ⋯ & 0 & f(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFirst, note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({J}_{1}(3)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{3}(5))}^{2} & ={([\\begin{aligned}{J}_{1}(3) & 0 \\\\ 0 & {J}_{3}(5)\\end{aligned}])}^{2} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{({J}_{1}(3))}^{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {({J}_{3}(5))}^{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={({J}_{1}(3))}^{2}\\,⊕\\,{({J}_{3}(5))}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we compute the function of each Jordan block separately.\n\n- For ${J}_{1}(3),$ we have $n=1$ and $\\lambda =3.$ since $n=1,$ we don't need any derivatives. So, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f({J}_{1}(3)) & =[\\begin{aligned}f(3)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{3}^{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}9\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For ${J}_{3}(5),$ we have $n=3$ and $\\lambda =2.$ \\since $n=3,$ we need to compute the first and second derivatives of $f$ only:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={x}^{2} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =2x \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f({J}_{3}(5)) & =[\\begin{aligned}f(5) & {f}^{′}(5) & \\frac{{f}^{″}(5)}{2} \\\\ 0 & f(5) & {f}^{′}(5) \\\\ 0 & 0 & f(5)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{5}^{2} & 2\\cdot 5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & {5}^{2} & 2\\cdot 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & {5}^{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}25 & 10 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 25 & 10 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 25\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({J}_{1}(3)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{3}(5)) & =({J}_{1}(3))\\,⊕\\,({J}_{3}(5)) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 25 & 10 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 25 & 10 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 25\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363672 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363673 13194