92186363745 1072 #uuid "e64c2e40-5741-5178-904b-8ae1b402b5d2" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363745 1074 "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $9{e}^{4x},$ and ${e}^{4x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, so we assume that a particular solution takes the form \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha x{e}^{4x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nWe calculate the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ using the product rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =(\\alpha x{e}^{4x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha (x{e}^{4x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha ({e}^{4x}+4x{e}^{4x}) \\\\  & =\\alpha (1+4x){e}^{4x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha (1+4x){e}^{4x})-4\\alpha x{e}^{4x} & =9{e}^{4x} \\\\ (1+4x-4x)\\alpha {e}^{4x} & =9{e}^{4x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{4x} & =9{e}^{4x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=9x{e}^{4x}.\n$$" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363746 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363747 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363746 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363746 1077 "field-1" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363746 1079 #edb/ref 17592186363747 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363746 1080 #uuid "9c09edc1-d4e2-5047-b686-22ff7a25e412" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363747 1081 "9x{e}^{4x}" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363747 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363747 1084 #uuid "0d24b86c-53c5-54d0-b886-505e14a28f45" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363748 1068 #edb/ref 17592186363749 13194