aec-554f-a3e7-85098a29b3ad" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363773 1081 "v(t)=2.5\\operatorname{tanh}⁡(4.9t)" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363773 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363773 1084 #uuid "40c1d5a3-51f1-546c-bd82-ac1a1c6efcff" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363774 1081 "v(t)=2.5\\operatorname{tanh}⁡(6.8t)" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363774 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363774 1084 #uuid "60459be5-5acd-5ec4-8b25-53fdf7862732" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363775 1081 "v(t)=2.5\\operatorname{tanh}⁡(2.54t)" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363775 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363775 1084 #uuid "ef2d23a6-ab0e-55b2-97ae-09c6899963b3" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363776 1081 "v(t)=2.5\\operatorname{tanh}⁡(24.5t)" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363776 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363776 1084 #uuid "6fe0dd73-4f03-5400-a2dd-168c9d466483" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363777 1068 #edb/ref 17592186363778 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363777 1069 "q-334967" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363777 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363777 1071 "A research buoy of mass $4\\,\\text{kg}$ is released from a platform and falls vertically downward with an initial velocity of $2.8\\,\\text{m/s}.$ Let $v(t)$ be the buoy's velocity, in meters per second, $t\\ge 0$ seconds after release, with downward taken as the positive direction. The air resistance acting on the buoy is negligible, and the only other force acting on it is its weight due to gravity.\n\nFind the equation for $v(t).$ Express your answer in terms of time $t$ and acceleration due to gravity $g.$\n\n$v(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363777 1072 #uuid "4c77db7c-70e0-5c92-8742-5d6e2b2a3afc" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363777 1074 "Recall Newton's second law, which states that the resultant force $F$ acting on a body equals the product of its mass $m$ and acceleration $a:$\n\n$$\nF=ma\n$$\n\nSince air resistance is negligible, the only force acting on the buoy is its weight due to gravity. The weight $w$ of the buoy is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =w \\\\  & =mg \\\\  & =4g.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that acceleration is the derivative of velocity: $a=\\frac{dv}{dt}.$  Substituting this into Newton's second law, along with our expression for the resultant force on the buoy, we get\n\n$$\n4g=4\\,\\frac{dv}{dt}.\n$$\n\nSimplifying and writing in standard form, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{dv}{dt}=g.\n$$\n\nNext, we integrate to solve for $v(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{dv}{dt}\\,dt=g\\int dt\n$$\n\n$$\nv(t)=gt+C\n$$\n\nNote that $C$ is a constant of integration.  We're told that the initial velocity of the buoy is $2.8\\,\\text{m/s}.$ So, we apply the initial condition $v(0)=2.8$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\nv(0)=g\\cdot 0+C\n$$\n\n$$\n2.8=0+C\n$$\n\n$$\nC=2.8\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity of the buoy $t$ seconds after release is\n\n$$\nv(t)=gt+2.8.\n$$" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363778 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363779 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363778 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363778 1077 "field-1" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363778 1079 #edb/ref 17592186363779 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363778 1080 #uuid "da85f45d-b90b-583a-add9-dabfbfacbc6d" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363779 1081 "gt+2.8" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363779 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363779 1084 #uuid "b8a910c4-0f01-5ca2-bfbc-c7022a30631b" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363780 