86363786 1071 "A reconnaissance supply canister of mass $7\\,\\text{kg}$ is launched vertically downward with an initial velocity of $3.1\\,\\text{m/s}.$ Let $v(t)$ be the canister's velocity, in meters per second, $t\\ge 0$ seconds after release, with downward taken as the positive direction.\nThe air resistance acting on the canister is negligible, and the only other force acting on it is its weight due to gravity.\n\nWhat is the velocity of the canister after $5$ seconds? Assume that acceleration due to gravity is $g=9.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$\n\nThe velocity after $5$ seconds is\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{m/s}.$" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363786 1072 #uuid "319b53e6-31a2-50cd-ba68-0bf430c968e6" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363786 1074 "Recall Newton's second law, which states that the resultant force $F$ acting on a body equals the product of its mass $m$ and acceleration $a:$\n\n$$\nF=ma\n$$\n\nSince air resistance is negligible, the only force acting on the canister is its weight due to gravity. The weight $w$ of the canister is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =w \\\\  & =mg \\\\  & =7g.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that acceleration is the derivative of velocity: $a=\\frac{dv}{dt}.$  Substituting this into Newton's second law, along with our expression for the resultant force on the canister, we get\n\n$$\n7g=7\\,\\frac{dv}{dt}.\n$$\n\nSimplifying and writing in standard form, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{dv}{dt}=g.\n$$\n\nNext, we integrate to solve for $v(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{dv}{dt}\\,dt=g\\int dt\n$$\n\n$$\nv(t)=gt+C\n$$\n\nNote that $C$ is a constant of integration.  We're told that the initial velocity of the canister is $3.1\\,\\text{m/s}.$ So, we apply the initial condition $v(0)=3.1$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\nv(0)=g\\cdot 0+C\n$$\n\n$$\n3.1=0+C\n$$\n\n$$\nC=3.1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity of the canister $t$ seconds after release is\n\n$$\nv(t)=gt+3.1.\n$$\n\nFinally, the velocity after $5$ seconds is\n\n$$\nv(5)=9.8\\cdot 5+3.1=52.1\\,\\text{m/s}.\n$$" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363787 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363788 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363787 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363787 1077 "field-1" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363787 1079 #edb/ref 17592186363788 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363787 1080 #uuid "6a54b652-07e9-513d-b4ad-f67313bdcc74" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363788 1081 "52.1" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363788 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363788 1084 #uuid "12524418-54f5-5694-a948-79f9ee9e75ce" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363789 1068 #edb/ref 17592186363790 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363789 1069 "q-335140" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363789 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363789 1071 "A supply pod of mass $30\\,\\text{kg}$ free-falls vertically downward. Let $v(t)$ be the pod's velocity, in meters per second, $t\\ge 0$ seconds after the descent began, with downward taken as the positive direction.\nThe air resistance on the pod is proportional to its velocity with a constant of proportionality $k>0$ and acts in the direction opposite to motion.\n\nWhich differential equation best models the velocity? Assume that the acceleration due to gravity is $g,$ and the only forces acting on the pod are the weight due to gravity and drag." 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363789 1072 #uuid "d40df24f-7f29-5d88-8be4-40799eaa2f95" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363789 1074 "Recall Newton's second law, which states that the resultant force $F$ acting on a body equals the product of its mass $m$ and acceleration $a:$\n\n$$\nF=ma\n$$\n\nIn our case, there are two forces acting on the pod:\n\n- The force due to gravity, given by the weight $w$ of the pod, which equals\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}w & =mg \\\\  & =30g\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The force due to drag (air resistance) $D,$ which is proportional to the velocity of the pod (and negative since it acts in the negative direction):\n\n$$\nD=-kv,\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality. Note that, since the drag, in $N=\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m/s}}^{2},$ is the product of the constant $k$ and velocity, in $\\text{m/s},$ the units of $k$ are\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{N}}{\\text{m/s}}=\\frac{\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m/s}}^{2}}{\\text{m/s}}=\\text{kg/s}.\n$$\n\nSumming the forces, the resultant force acting on the pod is\n\n$$\nF=w+D=30g-kv.\n$$\n\nAlso, recall that acceleration is the derivative of velocity: $a=\\frac{dv}{dt}.$\nSubstituting this into Newton's second law, along with our expression for the resultant force on the pod, we have\n\n$$\n30g-kv=30\\,\\frac{dv}{dt}.\n$$\n\nFinally, simplifying and writing in standard form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}30\\,\\frac{dv}{dt}+kv & =30g \\\\ \\frac{dv}{dt}+\\frac{k}{30}v & =g.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363790 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363791 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363790 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363792 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363790 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363793 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363790 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363794 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363790 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363795 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363790 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363790 1077 "selection" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363790 1079 #edb/ref 17592186363793 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363790 1080 #uuid "0d61b3c6-2d6e-50da-a73b-ad5936b17de9" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363791 1081 "\\frac{dv}{dt}-\\frac{k}{30}=g" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363791 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533446 17592186045