n.\n\nWhich differential equation best models the velocity? Assume the acceleration due to gravity is $g$, and the only forces acting on the crate are the weight due to gravity and drag." 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363803 1072 #uuid "bd9ee727-424f-50a8-bf00-3b9abd9fc26d" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363803 1074 "Recall Newton's second law, which states that the resultant force $F$ acting on a body equals the product of its mass $m$ and acceleration $a:$\n\n$$\nF=ma\n$$\n\nIn our case, there are two forces acting on the test dummy:\n\n- The force due to gravity, given by the weight $w$ of the dummy, which equals\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}w & =mg \\\\  & =55g\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The force due to drag (air resistance) $D,$ which is proportional to the velocity of the dummy (and negative since it acts in the negative direction):\n\n$$\nD=-kv=-22v\n$$\n\nSumming the forces, the resultant force acting on the dummy is\n\n$$\nF=w+D=55g-22v.\n$$\n\nAlso, recall that acceleration is the derivative of velocity: $a=\\frac{dv}{dt}.$\nSubstituting this into Newton's second law, along with our expression for the resultant force on the dummy, we have\n\n$$\n55g-22v=55\\,\\frac{dv}{dt}.\n$$\n\nFinally, simplifying and writing in standard form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}55\\,\\frac{dv}{dt}+22v & =55g \\\\ \\frac{dv}{dt}+\\frac{2}{5}v & =g.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363804 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363805 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363804 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363806 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363804 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363807 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363804 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363808 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363804 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363809 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363804 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363804 1077 "selection" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363804 1079 #edb/ref 17592186363805 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363804 1080 #uuid "4c5e4209-64cc-53a5-b038-5672f0be2d75" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363805 1081 "\\frac{dv}{dt}+\\frac{2}{5}v=g" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363805 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363805 1084 #uuid "d5b4c538-9c68-5684-a61e-8c8a2c92c100" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363806 1081 "\\frac{dv}{dt}-\\frac{2}{5}=g" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363806 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363806 1084 #uuid "c928ca3f-a156-5c43-9aaa-41c99efcbef7" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363807 1081 "\\frac{dv}{dt}-\\frac{5}{2}v=g" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363807 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363807 1084 #uuid "29a1c91c-fef5-5b6c-8426-f085f5feaab3" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363808 1081 "\\frac{dv}{dt}+\\frac{5}{2}v=g" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363808 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363808 1084 #uuid "2fdf074b-ccb6-5fe3-bffe-516ad6f6f24f" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363809 1081 "\\frac{dv}{dt}-\\frac{2}{5}v=g" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363809 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363809 1084 #uuid "82d0aeb0-53a3-57f2-8a11-85ac2c3be047" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363810 1068 #edb/ref 17592186363811 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363810 1069 "q-335184" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363810 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363810 1071 "A research module of mass $20\\,\\text{kg}$ is released from rest and falls vertically downward. Let $v(t)$ be the module's velocity, in meters per second, $t\\ge 0$ seconds after release, with downward taken as the positive direction.\nThe air resistance on the module is proportional to its velocity and acts in the direction opposite to motion.\nAt a particular moment during the fall, the module’s velocity is $10\\,\\text{m/s}$ and its instantaneous acceleration is $8.3\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$ Assume the acceleration due to gravity is $g=9.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2},$ and the only forces acting on the module are the weight due to gravity and drag.\n\nFind the equation for the velocity $v(t),$ expressing your answer in terms of $t$ **only.**\n\n$v(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363810 1072 #uuid "10841404-7237-599d-a213-842eefc7c119" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363810 1074 "First, note that only two forces act on the body: the force due to gravity, given by the weight\n\n$$\nw=mg=20\\cdot 9.8=196\\,\\text{N},\n$$\n\nand the force due to drag $D,$ which is proportional to the velocity (and negative since it acts in the negative direction),\n\n$$\nD=-kv,\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality. Hence, the resultant force acting on the body is\n\n$$\nF=w+D=196-kv.\n$$\n\nApplying Newton's second law, we obtain an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =ma \\\\ 196-kv & =20\\,\\frac{dv}{dt} \\\\ \\frac{dv}{dt}+\\frac{k}{20}v & =9.8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that, at a particular moment, the body’s velocity is $10\\,\\text{m/s}$ and its instantaneous acceleration is $8.3\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$ Substituting these values into the ODE, we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n8.3+\\frac{k}{20}\\cdot 10=9.8\\,⟹\\,k=3\\,\\text{kg/s}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\frac{dv}{dt}+\\frac{3}{20}v=9.8\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${v}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{v}_{c}}{dt}+\\frac{3}{20}{v}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{v}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-3t/20},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{v}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${v}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{v}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+\\frac{3}{20}\\alpha =9.8\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =\\frac{196}{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${v}_{p}(t)=\\frac{196}{3}.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nv(t)={v}_{c}(t)+{v}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-3t/20}+\\frac{196}{3}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the body is released from rest. So, we can apply the initial condition $v(0)=0,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(0) & =A{e}^{-3\\cdot 0/20}+\\frac{196}{3} \\\\ 0 & =A+\\frac{196}{3} \\\\ A & =-\\frac{196}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =-\\frac{196}{3}{e}^{-3t/20}+\\frac{196}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{196}{3}(1-{e}^{-3t/20})\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363811 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363812 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363811 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363811 1077 "field-1" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363811 1079 #edb/ref 17592186363812 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363811 1080 #uuid "b9662023-9fa6-55a9-9df5-d989be3fece1" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363812 1081 "(196/3)(1-{e}^{-3t/20})" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [1759