20-95c1-897f9ae79cca" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363821 1081 "34.5" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363821 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363821 1084 #uuid "e8b35a95-5201-51c7-b530-c707d78375cd" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363822 1068 #edb/ref 17592186363823 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363822 1069 "q-335311" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363822 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363822 1071 "A supply package of mass $45\\,\\text{kg}$ falls vertically downward from a helicopter. Let $v(t)$ be the package's velocity, in meters per second, $t\\ge 0$ seconds after the fall began, with downward taken as the positive direction.\nThe air resistance on the package is proportional to the square of the package's velocity and acts in the direction opposite to motion.\n\nWhich differential equation best models the velocity? Assume that $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, the acceleration due to gravity is $g,$ and the only forces acting on the package are the weight due to gravity and drag." 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363822 1072 #uuid "8d917bbd-b922-5d2c-8210-f6bad75daf61" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363822 1074 "Recall Newton's second law, which states that the resultant force $F$ acting on a body equals the product of its mass $m$ and acceleration $a:$\n\n$$\nF=ma\n$$\n\nIn our case, two forces are acting on the package:\n\n- The force due to gravity, given by the weight $w$ of the package, which equals\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}w & =mg \\\\  & =45g.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The force due to drag (air resistance) $D,$ which is proportional to the square of the velocity of the package (and negative since it acts in the negative direction):\n\n$$\nD=-k{v}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality. Note that, since drag, in $\\text{N}=\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m/s}}^{2},$ is the product of the constant $k$ and squared velocity, in $(\\text{m/s})^{2}={\\text{m}}^{2}/{\\text{s}}^{2},$ the units of $k$ are\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{N}}{{\\text{m}}^{2}/{\\text{s}}^{2}}=\\frac{\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m/s}}^{2}}{{\\text{m}}^{2}/{\\text{s}}^{2}}=\\text{kg/m}.\n$$\n\nSumming the forces, the resultant force acting on the package is\n\n$$\nF=w+D=45g-k{v}^{2}.\n$$\n\nAlso, recall that acceleration is the derivative of velocity: $a=\\frac{dv}{dt}.$\nSubstituting this into Newton's second law, along with our expression for the resultant force on the package, we get\n\n$$\n45g-k{v}^{2}=45\\,\\frac{dv}{dt}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{dv}{dt}=g-\\frac{k}{45}{v}^{2}.\n$$" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363823 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363824 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363823 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363825 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363823 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363826 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363823 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363827 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363823 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363828 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363823 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363823 1077 "selection" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363823 1079 #edb/ref 17592186363826 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363823 1080 #uuid "c36eaf3c-313d-5d7d-9a66-1a3ee0c928cc" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363824 1081 "\\frac{dv}{dt}=g-\\frac{k}{45}v" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363824 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363824 1084 #uuid "036f4be8-c8ad-5094-809b-cdb4ff607974" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363825 1081 "\\frac{dv}{dt}=45g-kv" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363825 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363825 1084 #uuid "185cdc87-977e-5bd8-a625-23bad157b8bc" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363826 1081 "\\frac{dv}{dt}=g-\\frac{k}{45}{v}^{2}" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363826 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363826 1084 #uuid "089986cb-dbc8-5e41-886d-f523d9c1f8a1" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363827 1081 "\\frac{dv}{dt}=45g-k{v}^{2}" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363827 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363827 1084 #uuid "97850765-8b03-55b5-ae75-c4c9ef9ca809" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363828 1081 "\\frac{dv}{dt}=kg-45{v}^{2}" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363828 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363828 1084 #uuid "7c09eea2-4940-5925-9f6e-bc84ca481b0f" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363829 1068 #edb/ref 17592186363830 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363829 1069 "q-335324" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363829 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363829 1071 "A body of mass $50\\,\\text{kg}$ falls vertically downward. Let $v$ be its velocity, with downward taken as the positive direction.\nThe air resistance on the body is proportional to its velocity with a constant of proportionality $k=10\\,\\text{kg/s}$ and acts opposite to the direction of motion.\n\nWhat is the terminal velocity ${v}_{\\text{term}}$ of the body? Assume the acceleration due to gravity is $g=9.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2},$ and the only forces acting on the body are the weight due to gravity and drag.\n\n${v}_{\\text{term}}=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{m/s}$" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363829 1072 #uuid "61cf5e94-a6d3-52e1-8a41-7864d403aee5" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363829 1074 "Terminal velocity is the constant speed a falling object approaches as the upward resistive force (drag) grows to exactly balance the downward gravitational force.\nThat is, at terminal velocity (a constant), acceleration is zero, so by $F=ma,$ the resultant force is zero.\n\nFirst, note that only two forces act on the body: the force due to gravity, given by the weight\n\n$$\nw=mg=50\\cdot 9.8=490\\,\\text{N},\n$$\n\nand the force due to drag $D,$ which is proportional to the velocity (and negative since it acts in the negative direction),\n\n$$\nD=-kv=-10v.\n$$\n\nHence, the resultant force acting on the body is\n\n$$\nF=w+D=490-10v.\n$$\n\nWhen the body reaches terminal velocity ${v}_{\\text{term}},$ the resultant force is zero:\n\n$$\n490-10{v}_{\\text{term}}=0\\,⟹\\,{v}_{\\text{term}}=\\frac{490}{10}=49\n$$\n\nTherefore, the terminal velocity of the body is ${v}_{\\text{term}}=49\\,\\text{m/s}.$" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363830 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363831 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363830 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363830 1077 "field-1" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363830 1079 #edb/ref 17592186363831 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363830 1080 #uuid "e2bb1c30-62db-5c3d-b372-03f2aa084e04" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363831 1081 "49" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363831 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363831 1084 #uuid "6e305c58-5dbe-5a92-b826-3ad8939d2ea7" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363832 1068 #edb/ref 17592186363833 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363832 1069 "q-335325" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363832 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363832 1071 "A wooden box of mass $20\\,\\text{kg}$ falls vertically downward. Let $v(t)$ be the wooden box's velocity, in meters per second, $t\\ge 0$ seconds after the descent began, with downward taken as the positive direction.\nThe air resistance on the wooden box is proportional to the square of its velocity with a constant of proportionality $k=\\frac{2}{25}\\,\\text{kg/m}$ and acts in the direction opposite to motion.\n\nWhich differential equation best models the velocity? Assume the acceleration due to gravity is $g,$ and the only forces acting on the wooden box are the weight due to gravity and drag." 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363832 1072 #uuid "e963ed41-c473-5a41-bb46-b16bf4bcf648" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363832 1074 "Recall Newton's second law, which states that the resultant force $F$ acting on a body equals the product of its mass $m$ and acceleration $a:$\n\n$$\nF=ma\n$$\n\nIn our case, two forces are acting on the wooden box:\n\n- The force due to gravity, given by the weight $w$ of the wooden box, which equals\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}w & =mg \\\\  & =20g.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The force due to drag (air resistance) $D,$ which is proportional to the square of the velocity of the wooden box (and negative since it acts in the negative direction):\n\n$$\nD=-k{v}^{2}=-\\frac{2}{25}{v}^{2}\n$$\n\nSumming the forces, the resultant force acting on the wooden box is\n\n$$\nF=w+D=20g-\\frac{2}{25}{v}^{2}.\n$$\n\nAlso, recall that acceleration is the derivative of velocity: $a=\\frac{dv}{dt}.$\nSubstituting this into Newton's second law, along with our expression for the resultant force on the wooden box, we have\n\n$$\n20g-\\frac{2}{25}{v}^{2}=20\\,\\frac{dv}{dt}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{dv}{dt}=g-\\frac{1}{250}{v}^{2}.\n$$" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363833 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363834 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363833 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363835 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363833 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363836 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363833 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363837 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363833 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363838 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363833 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363833 1077 "selection" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363833 1079 #edb/ref 17592186363838 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363833 1080 #uuid "4cb98d76-3a4b-5350-9f03-0b9e0acb0772" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363834 1081 "\\frac{dv}{dt}=250g-v" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363834 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363834 1084 #uuid "167bf9e8-2b82-5d81-8f36-1d0de7993ded" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363835 1081 "\\frac{dv}{dt}=g-250{v}^{2}" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363835 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363835 1084 #uuid "58d2e7d6-e700-535d-8926-e413fa43d554" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363836 1081 "\\frac{dv}{dt}=250g-{v}^{2}" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363836 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363836 1084 #uuid "6f93f813-a592-5b47-9018-542ad0bcf506" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363837 1081 "\\frac{dv}{dt}=g-\\frac{1}{250}v" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363837 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363837 1084 #uuid "455a9324-f992-566c-ae34-e53ef7c23488" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363838 1081 "\\frac{dv}{dt}=g-\\frac{1}{250}{v}^{2}" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363838 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363838 1084 #uuid "151cb0ee-f37e-5525-8ed1-12a33f747237" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363839 1068 #edb/ref 17592186363840 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363839 1069 "q-335332" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363839 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363839 1071 "A body of mass $12\\,\\text{kg}$ falls vertically downward. Let $v$ be its velocity, with downward taken as the positive direction.\nThe air resistance on the body is proportional to its velocity and acts in the opposite direction to the motion.\nAt a particular moment during the fall, the body’s velocity is $10\\,\\text{m/s}$ and its instantaneous acceleration is $6.3\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$\n\nWhat is the terminal velocity ${v}_{\\text{term}}$ of the body? Assume the acceleration due to gravity is $g=9.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2},$ and the only forces acting on the body are the weight due to gravity and drag.\n\n${v}_{\\text{term}}=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{m/s}$" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363839 1072 #uuid "defee367-190b-5c38-a47e-ac69223e9459" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363839 1074 "Terminal velocity is the constant speed a falling object approaches as the upward resistive force (drag) grows to exactly balance the downward gravitational force.\nThat is, at terminal velocity (a constant), acceleration is zero, so by $F=ma,$ the resultant force is zero.\n\nFirst, note that only two forces act on the body: the force due to gravity, given by the weight\n\n$$\nw=mg=12\\cdot 9.8=117.6\\,\\text{N},\n$$\n\nand the force due to drag $D,$ which is proportional to the velocity (and negative since it acts in the negative direction),\n\n$$\nD=-kv,\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality. Hence, the resultant force acting on the body is\n\n$$\nF=w+D=117.6-kv.\n$$\n\nApplying Newton's second law, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =ma \\\\ 117.6-kv & =12a\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that, at a particular moment, the body’s velocity is $10\\,\\text{m/s}$ and its instantaneous acceleration is $6.3\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$ Substituting these values into the above, we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}117.6-10k & =12\\cdot 6.3 \\\\ 117.6-10k & =75.6 \\\\ -10k & =-42 \\\\ k & =4.2\\,\\text{kg/s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the resultant force acting on the body is\n\n$$\nF=117.6-4.2v.\n$$\n\nWhen the body reaches terminal velocity ${v}_{\\text{term}},$ the resultant force is zero:\n\n$$\n117.6-4.2{v}_{\\text{term}}=0\\,⟹\\,{v}_{\\text{term}}=\\frac{117.6}{4.2}=28\n$$\n\nTherefore, the terminal velocity of the body is ${v}_{\\text{term}}=28\\,\\text{m/s}.$" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363840 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363841 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363840 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363840 1077 "field-1" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363840 1079 #edb/ref 17592186363841 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363840 1080 #uuid "c503a542-4769-578f-8a2e-e84bf887bc52" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363841 1081 "28" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363841 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363841 1084 #uuid "74eb6124-52ec-59e5-acfe-eb63a05b7f8d" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363842 1068 #edb/ref 17592186363843 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363842 1069 "q-335347" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363842 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363842 1071 "A wooden crate of mass $14\\,\\text{kg}$ falls vertically downward. Let $v$ be its velocity, with downward taken as the positive direction.\nThe air resistance on the crate is proportional to its velocity and acts in the opposite direction to the motion.\nAt a particular moment during the fall, the crate’s velocity is $7\\,\\text{m/s}$ and its instantaneous acceleration is $6.3\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$\n\nWhat is the terminal velocity ${v}_{\\text{term}}$ of the crate? Assume the acceleration due to gravity is $g=9.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2},$ and the only forces acting on the crate are the weight due to gravity and drag.\n\n${v}_{\\text{term}}=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{m/s}$" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363842 1072 #uuid "599fa805-5ce9-549e-a9e8-aa48d09d2895" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363842 1074 "Terminal velocity is the constant speed a falling object approaches as the upward resistive force (drag) grows to exactly balance the downward gravitational force.\nThat is, at terminal velocity (a constant), acceleration is zero, so by $F=ma,$ the resultant force is zero.\n\nFirst, note that only two forces act on the crate: the force due to gravity, given by the weight\n\n$$\nw=mg=14\\cdot 9.8=137.2\\,\\text{N},\n$$\n\nand the force due to drag $D,$ which is proportional to the velocity (and negative since it acts in the negative direction),\n\n$$\nD=-kv,\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality. Hence, the resultant force acting on the crate is\n\n$$\nF=w+D=137.2-kv.\n$$\n\nApplying Newton's second law, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =ma \\\\ 137.2-kv & =14a\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that, at a particular moment, the crate’s velocity is $7\\,\\text{m/s}$ and its instantaneous acceleration is $6.3\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$ Substituting these values into the above, we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}137.2-7k & =14\\cdot 6.3 \\\\ 137.2-7k & =88.2 \\\\ -7k & =-49 \\\\ k & =7\\,\\text{kg/s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the resultant force acting on the crate is\n\n$$\nF=137.2-7v.\n$$\n\nWhen the crate reaches terminal velocity ${v}_{\\text{term}},$ the resultant force is zero:\n\n$$\n137.2-7{v}_{\\text{term}}=0\\,⟹\\,{v}_{\\text{term}}=\\frac{137.2}{7}=19.6\n$$\n\nTherefore, the terminal velocity of the crate is ${v}_{\\text{term}}=19.6\\,\\text{m/s}.$" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363843 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363844 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363843 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363843 1077 "field-1" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363843 1079 #edb/ref 17592186363844 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363843 1080 #uuid "066fd902-ac1a-5137-ae63-0a0e4b465028" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363844 1081 "19.6" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363844 1083 #edb