ting variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dv}{dt} & =g(1-0.0016{v}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{1}{1-0.0016{v}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{dv}{dt} & =g \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{1-0.0016{v}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{dv}{dt}\\,dt & =g\\int dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{1-(0.04v)^{2}}\\,dv & =g\\int dt \\\\ \\frac{1}{0.04}\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(0.04v) & =gt+{C}_{1} \\\\ \\operatorname{artanh}⁡(0.04v) & =\\frac{1}{25}gt+C \\\\ 0.04v & =\\operatorname{tanh}⁡(\\frac{1}{25}gt+C) \\\\ v(t) & =25\\operatorname{tanh}⁡(\\frac{1}{25}gt+C),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=0.04{C}_{1}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the structure is released with an initial velocity of $4\\,\\text{m/s}.$ So, we can apply the initial condition $v(0)=4,$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(0) & =25\\operatorname{tanh}⁡(\\frac{1}{25}g\\cdot 0+C) \\\\ 4 & =25\\operatorname{tanh}⁡(C) \\\\ \\operatorname{tanh}⁡(C) & =0.16 \\\\ C & =\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(0.16)≈0.161\\,38\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $v(t):$\n\n$$\nv(t)=25\\operatorname{tanh}⁡(\\frac{1}{25}gt+0.161\\,38)\n$$\n\nFinally, the velocity of the structure after $t=1$ second is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(1) & =25\\operatorname{tanh}⁡(\\frac{1}{25}\\cdot 9.8\\cdot 1+0.161\\,38) \\\\  & =25\\operatorname{tanh}⁡(0.392+0.161\\,38) \\\\  & =12.576\\,29… \\\\  & ≈12.58\\,\\text{m/s},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363881 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363882 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363881 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363883 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363881 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363884 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363881 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363885 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363881 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363886 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363881 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363881 1077 "selection" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363881 1079 #edb/ref 17592186363883 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363881 1080 #uuid "8b723983-8fdf-52f4-a0e9-8fbfefc187b9" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363882 1081 "11.27\\,\\text{m/s}" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363882 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363882 1084 #uuid "6d679b6d-0193-547c-91a2-ce68263e12fb" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363883 1081 "12.58\\,\\text{m/s}" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363883 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363883 1084 #uuid "016bc3b8-2579-54f7-b788-00c7324372ff" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363884 1081 "11.54\\,\\text{m/s}" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363884 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363884 1084 #uuid "15459ce4-2f10-5851-9fc2-89d63e846dd2" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363885 1081 "12.89\\,\\text{m/s}" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363885 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363885 1084 #uuid "dbb3168e-d19e-5a6e-a24d-a321d062596e" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363886 1081 "12.12\\,\\text{m/s}" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363886 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363886 1084 #uuid "9a2efa71-a0c2-5cd4-8f3a-37a7ed06dfaa" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363887 1068 #edb/ref 17592186363888 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363887 1069 "q-335736" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363887 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363887 1071 "A delivery pod of mass $28\\,\\text{kg}$ falls vertically downward. Let $v$ be its velocity, with downward taken as the positive direction.\nThe air resistance on the pod is proportional to its velocity with a constant of proportionality $k=7\\,\\text{kg/s}$ and acts opposite to the direction of motion.\n\nWhat is the terminal velocity ${v}_{\\text{term}}$ of the pod? Assume the acceleration due to gravity is $g=9.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2},$ and the only forces acting on the pod are the weight due to gravity and drag.\n\n${v}_{\\text{term}}=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{m/s}$" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363887 1072 #uuid "d3e95754-af7f-5b77-a99f-13be6c803abd" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363887 1074 "Terminal velocity is the constant speed a falling object approaches as the upward resistive force (drag) grows to exactly balance the downward gravitational force.\nThat is, at terminal velocity (a constant), acceleration is zero, so by $F=ma,$ the resultant force is zero.\n\nFirst, note that only two forces act on the pod: the force due to gravity, given by the weight\n\n$$\nw=mg=28\\cdot 9.8=274.4\\,\\text{N},\n$$\n\nand the force due to drag $D,$ which is proportional to the velocity (and negative since it acts in the negative direction),\n\n$$\nD=-kv=-7v.\n$$\n\nHence, the resultant force acting on the pod is\n\n$$\nF=w+D=274.4-7v.\n$$\n\nWhen the pod reaches terminal velocity ${v}_{\\text{term}},$ the resultant force is zero:\n\n$$\n274.4-7{v}_{\\text{term}}=0\\,⟹\\,{v}_{\\text{term}}=\\frac{274.4}{7}=39.2\n$$\n\nTherefore, the terminal velocity of the pod is ${v}_{\\text{term}}=39.2\\,\\text{m/s}.$" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363888 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363889 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363888 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363888 1077 "field-1" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363888 1079 #edb/ref 17592186363889 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363888 1080 #uuid "50a9ee99-0f47-5b98-bb53-cd7512ac32a7" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363889 1081 "39.2" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363889 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363889 1084 #uuid "f4f3e257-fa08-5a9c-8fd0-ddb9b6fab0be" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363890 1068 #edb/ref 17592186363891 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363890 1069 "q-335757" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363890 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363890 1071 "A snow sensor of mass $12\\,\\text{kg}$ falls vertically downward. Let $v(t)$ be the sensor’s velocity, in meters per second, $t\\ge 0$ seconds after the fall began, with downward taken as the positive direction.\nThe air resistance acting on the sensor is negligible, and the only other force acting on it is the weight due to gravity. Which differential equation best models the velocity? Assume the acceleration due to gravity is $g.$" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363890 1072 #uuid "7052f414-ee05-561a-a39c-520f52217183" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363890 1074 "Recall Newton's second law, which states that the resultant force $F$ acting on a body equals the product of its mass $m$ and acceleration $a:$\n\n$$\nF=ma\n$$\n\nSince air resistance is negligible, the only force acting on the sensor is its weight due to gravity. The weight $w$ of the sensor is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =w \\\\  & =mg \\\\  & =12g.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that acceleration is the derivative of velocity: $a=\\frac{dv}{dt}.$\nSubstituting this into Newton's second law, along with our expression for the resultant force on the sensor, we get\n\n$$\n12g=12\\,\\frac{dv}{dt}\n$$\n\nFinally, simplifying and writing in standard form, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{dv}{dt}=g.\n$$" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363891 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363892 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363891 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363893 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363891 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363894 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363891 1075 #