39533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363897 1071 "A sack of mass $50\\,\\text{kg}$ is released from rest and falls vertically downward. Let $v(t)$ be the sack's velocity, in meters per second, $t\\ge 0$ seconds after release, with downward taken as the positive direction.\nThe air resistance on the sack is proportional to the square of its velocity and acts in the direction opposite to motion.\nAt a particular moment during the fall, the sack’s velocity is $2.5\\,\\text{m/s}$ and its instantaneous acceleration is $7.35\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$\n\nFind the equation for the velocity $v(t).$\nAssume the acceleration due to gravity is $g=9.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2},$ and the only forces acting on the sack are the weight due to gravity and drag." 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363897 1072 #uuid "003ffa79-2405-5b8d-a3f9-1e9ba87b2c52" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363897 1074 "First, note that only two forces act on the sack: the force due to gravity, given by the weight\n\n$$\nw=mg=50g\n$$\n\nand the force due to drag $D,$ which is proportional to the velocity squared (and negative since it acts in the negative direction),\n\n$$\nD=-k{v}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality. Hence, the resultant force acting on the sack is\n\n$$\nF=w+D=50g-k{v}^{2}.\n$$\n\nApplying Newton's second law, we obtain a separable first-order differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =ma \\\\ 50g-k{v}^{2} & =50\\,\\frac{dv}{dt} \\\\ \\frac{dv}{dt} & =g-\\frac{k}{50}{v}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that, at a particular moment, the sack's velocity is $2.5\\,\\text{m/s}$ and its instantaneous acceleration is $7.35\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$ Substituting these values and $g=9.8$ into the ODE, we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n7.35=g-\\frac{k}{50}\\cdot {2.5}^{2}\\,⟹\\,k=19.6\\,\\text{kg/m}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\frac{dv}{dt}=g-0.392{v}^{2}\n$$\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dv}{dt} & =g(1-0.04{v}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{1}{1-0.04{v}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{dv}{dt} & =g \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{1-0.04{v}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{dv}{dt}\\,dt & =g\\int dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{1-(0.2v)^{2}}\\,dv & =g\\int dt \\\\ \\frac{1}{0.2}\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(0.2v) & =gt+{C}_{1} \\\\ \\operatorname{artanh}⁡(0.2v) & =\\frac{1}{5}gt+C \\\\ 0.2v & =\\operatorname{tanh}⁡(\\frac{1}{5}gt+C) \\\\ v(t) & =5\\operatorname{tanh}⁡(\\frac{1}{5}gt+C)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=0.2{C}_{1}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the body is released from rest. So, we can apply the initial condition $v(0)=0,$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(0) & =5\\operatorname{tanh}⁡(\\frac{1}{5}g\\cdot 0+C) \\\\ 0 & =5\\operatorname{tanh}⁡(C) \\\\ \\operatorname{tanh}⁡(C) & =0 \\\\ C & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $v(t):$\n\n$$\nv(t)=5\\operatorname{tanh}⁡(\\frac{1}{5}gt)\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating $\\frac{1}{5}g,$ we have\n\n$$\nv(t)=5\\operatorname{tanh}⁡(1.96t).\n$$" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363898 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363899 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363898 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363900 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363898 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363901 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363898 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363902 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363898 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363903 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363898 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363898 1077 "selection" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363898 1079 #edb/ref 17592186363899 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363898 1080 #uuid "6fabcfa7-5f73-520b-88fd-e8a3c0495ae1" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363899 1081 "v(t)=5\\operatorname{tanh}⁡(1.96t)" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363899 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533446 1759