lying Newton's second law, we obtain a separable first-order differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =ma \\\\ 5\\,000g-k{v}^{2} & =5\\,000\\,\\frac{dv}{dt} \\\\ \\frac{dv}{dt} & =g-\\frac{k}{5\\,000}{v}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that, at a particular moment, the container's velocity is $12.5\\,\\text{m/s}$ and its instantaneous acceleration is $7.35\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$ Substituting these values and $g=9.8$ into the ODE, we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n7.35=g-\\frac{k}{5\\,000}\\cdot (12.5)^{2}\\,⟹\\,k=78.4\\,\\text{kg/m}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\frac{dv}{dt}=g-0.01568{v}^{2}\n$$\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dv}{dt} & =g(1-0.0016{v}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{1}{1-0.0016{v}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{dv}{dt} & =g \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{1-0.0016{v}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{dv}{dt}\\,dt & =g\\int dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{1-(0.04v)^{2}}\\,dv & =g\\int dt \\\\ \\frac{1}{0.04}\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(0.04v) & =gt+{C}_{1} \\\\ \\operatorname{artanh}⁡(0.04v) & =\\frac{1}{25}gt+C \\\\ 0.04v & =\\operatorname{tanh}⁡(\\frac{1}{25}gt+C) \\\\ v(t) & =25\\operatorname{tanh}⁡(\\frac{1}{25}gt+C)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=0.04{C}_{1}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the container is released from rest. So, we can apply the initial condition $v(0)=0,$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(0) & =25\\operatorname{tanh}⁡(\\frac{1}{25}g\\cdot 0+C) \\\\ 0 & =25\\operatorname{tanh}⁡(C) \\\\ \\operatorname{tanh}⁡(C) & =0 \\\\ C & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $v(t):$\n\n$$\nv(t)=25\\operatorname{tanh}⁡(\\frac{1}{25}gt)\n$$\n\nFinally, the velocity of the container after $t=3$ seconds is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(3) & =25\\operatorname{tanh}⁡(0.392\\cdot 3) \\\\  & =20.6547… \\\\  & ≈20.65\\,\\text{m/s},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363919 107