9533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363925 1068 #edb/ref 17592186363926 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363925 1069 "q-336169" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363925 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363925 1071 "An object of mass $25\\,\\text{kg}$ falls vertically downward. Let $v$ be its velocity, with downward taken as the positive direction.\nThe air resistance on the object is proportional to the square of its velocity with a constant of proportionality $k=5\\,\\text{kg/m}$ and acts opposite to the direction of motion.\n\nWhat is the terminal velocity ${v}_{\\text{term}}$ of the object? Assume the acceleration due to gravity is $g=9.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2},$ and the only forces acting on the object are the weight due to gravity and drag.\n\n${v}_{\\text{term}}=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{m/s}$" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363925 1072 #uuid "b88f0814-714f-56af-823b-e32614cede44" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363925 1074 "Terminal velocity is the constant speed a falling object approaches as the upward resistive force (drag) grows to exactly balance the downward gravitational force.\nThat is, at terminal velocity (a constant), acceleration is zero, so by $F=ma,$ the resultant force is zero.\n\nFirst, note that only two forces act on the object: the force due to gravity, given by the weight\n\n$$\nw=mg=25\\cdot 9.8=245\\,\\text{N},\n$$\n\nand the force due to drag $D,$ which is proportional to the square of the velocity (and negative since it acts in the negative direction),\n\n$$\nD=-k{v}^{2}=-5{v}^{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the resultant force acting on the object is\n\n$$\nF=w+D=245-5{v}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWhen the object reaches terminal velocity ${v}_{\\text{term}},$ the resultant force is zero:\n\n$$\n245-5({v}_{\\text{term}})^{2}=0\\,⟹\\,{v}_{\\text{term}}=\\sqrt{\\frac{245}{5}}=7\n$$\n\nTherefore, the terminal velocity of the object is ${v}_{\\text{term}}=7\\,\\text{m/s}.$" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363926 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363927 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363926 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363926 1077 "field-1" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363926 1079 #edb/ref 17592186363927 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363926 1080 #uuid "1a666180-e881-5c6b-b94a-77333c159f93" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363927 1081 "7" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363927 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363927 1084 #uuid "add70c62-fa5f-5b43-b1f5-a7b614f9eca2" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363928 1068 #edb/ref 17592186363929 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363928 1069 "q-336181" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363928 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363928 1071 "A stone of mass $15\\,\\text{kg}$ falls vertically downward. Let $v$ be its velocity, with downward taken as the positive direction.\nThe air resistance on the stone is proportional to the square of its velocity with a constant of proportionality $k=2\\,\\text{kg/m}$ and acts opposite to the direction of motion.\n\nWhat is the terminal velocity ${v}_{\\text{term}}$ of the stone? Assume the acceleration due to gravity is $g=9.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2},$ and the only forces acting on the stone are the weight due to gravity and drag.\n\nExpress your answer in the form $a\\sqrt{b},$ where $a$ and $b$ are rational numbers.\n\n${v}_{\\text{term}}=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{m/s}$" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363928 1072 #uuid "e90e6fd5-0d90-5e99-885b-c3b4d4bb346b" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363928 1074 "Terminal velocity is the constant speed a falling object approaches as the upward resistive force (drag) grows to exactly balance the downward gravitational force.\nThat is, at terminal velocity (a constant), acceleration is zero, so by $F=ma,$ the resultant force is zero.\n\nFirst, note that only two forces act on the stone: the force due to gravity, given by the weight\n\n$$\nw=mg=15\\cdot 9.8=147\\,\\text{N},\n$$\n\nand the force due to drag $D,$ which is proportional to the square of the velocity (and negative since it acts in the negative direction),\n\n$$\nD=-k{v}^{2}=-2{v}^{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the resultant force acting on the stone is\n\n$$\nF=w+D=147-2{v}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWhen the stone reaches terminal velocity ${v}_{\\text{term}},$ the resultant force is zero:\n\n$$\n147-2({v}_{\\text{term}})^{2}=0\\,⟹\\,{v}_{\\text{term}}=\\sqrt{\\frac{147}{2}}=\\frac{7\\sqrt{6}}{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the terminal velocity of the stone is ${v}_{\\text{term}}=\\frac{7\\sqrt{6}}{2}\\,\\text{m/s}.$" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363929 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363930 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363929 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363929 1077 "field-1" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363929 1079 #edb/ref 17592186363930 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363929 1080 #uuid "05530d09-46a5-5174-8e61-a5203ad05e6a" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363930 1081 "\\frac{7\\sqrt{6}}{2}" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363930 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363930 1084 #uuid "ec565be0-0413-5964-9d3a-4ee51fc80548" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363931 1068 #edb/ref 17592186363932 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363931 1069 "q-336194" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363931 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363931 1071 "A body of mass $40\\,\\text{kg}$ falls vertically downward. Let $v$ be its velocity, with downward taken as the positive direction.\nThe air resistance on the body is proportional to the square of its velocity and acts in the opposite direction to the direction of motion.\nAt a particular moment during the fall, the body's velocity is $2\\sqrt{5}\\,\\text{m/s}$ and its instantaneous acceleration is $\\frac{49}{10}\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$\n\nWhat is the terminal velocity ${v}_{\\text{term}}$ of the body? Assume the acceleration due to gravity is $g=9.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2},$ and the only forces acting on the ball are the weight due to gravity and drag. \n\nExpress your answer in the form $a\\sqrt{b},$ where $a$ and $b$ are integers.\n\n${v}_{\\text{term}}=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{m/s}$" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363931 1072 #uuid "3b36acf6-85ba-5aa3-a626-a02bb0d18d31" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363931 1074 "Terminal velocity is the constant speed a falling object approaches as the upward resistive force (drag) grows to exactly balance the downward gravitational force.\nThat is, at terminal velocity (a constant), acceleration is zero, so by $F=ma,$ the resultant force is zero.\n\nFirst, note that only two forces act on the body: the force due to gravity, given by the weight\n\n$$\nw=mg=40\\cdot 9.8=392\\,\\text{N},\n$$\n\nand the force due to drag $D,$ which is proportional to the square of the velocity (and negative since it acts in the negative direction),\n\n$$\nD=-k{v}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality. Hence, the resultant force acting on the body is\n\n$$\nF=w+D=392-k{v}^{2}.\n$$\n\nApplying Newton's second law, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =ma \\\\ 392-k{v}^{2} & =40a\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that, at a particular moment, the body's velocity is $2\\sqrt{5}\\,\\text{m/s}$ and its instantaneous acceleration is $\\frac{49}{10}\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$ Substituting these values into the above, we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}392-k\\cdot {(2\\sqrt{5})}^{2} & =40\\cdot \\frac{49}{10} \\\\ 392-20k & =196 \\\\ -20k & =-196 \\\\ k & =\\frac{196}{20} \\\\ k & =\\frac{49}{5}\\,\\text{kg/m}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the resultant force acting on the body is\n\n$$\nF=392-\\frac{49}{5}{v}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWhen the body reaches terminal velocity ${v}_{\\text{term}},$ the resultant force is zero:\n\n$$\n392-\\frac{49}{5}({v}_{\\text{term}})^{2}=0\\,⟹\\,{v}_{\\text{term}}=\\sqrt{40}=2\\sqrt{10}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the terminal velocity of the body is ${v}_{\\text{term}}=2\\sqrt{10}\\,\\text{m/s}.$" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363932 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363933 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363932 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363932 1077 "field-1" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363932 1079 #edb/ref 17592186363933 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363932 1080 #uuid "122d51cd-d2ab-5e50-9d8e-4c21b8e721a2" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363933 1081 "2\\sqrt{10}" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363933 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363933 1084 #uuid "96006349-4f1d-5031-9f12-998169077923" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363934 1068 #edb/ref 17592186363935 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363934 1069 "q-336197" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363934 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363934 1071 "A package of mass $100\\,\\text{kg}$ falls vertically downward. Let $v$ be its velocity, with downward taken as the positive direction.\nThe air resistance on the package is proportional to the square of its velocity and acts in the opposite direction to the direction of motion.\nAt a particular moment during the fall, the package's velocity is $\\sqrt{10}\\,\\text{m/s}$ and its instantaneous acceleration is $\\frac{49}{25}\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$\n\nWhat is the terminal velocity ${v}_{\\text{term}}$ of the package? Assume the acceleration due to gravity is $g=9.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2},$ and the only forces acting on the package are the weight due to gravity and drag. \n\nExpress