, at terminal velocity (a constant), acceleration is zero, so by $F=ma,$ the resultant force is zero.\n\nFirst, note that only two forces act on the bag: the force due to gravity, given by the weight\n\n$$\nw=mg=30\\cdot 9.8=294\\,\\text{N},\n$$\n\nand the force due to drag $D,$ which is proportional to the square of the velocity (and negative since it acts in the negative direction),\n\n$$\nD=-k{v}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality. Hence, the resultant force acting on the body is\n\n$$\nF=w+D=294-k{v}^{2}.\n$$\n\nApplying Newton's second law, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =ma \\\\ 294-k{v}^{2} & =30a\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that, at a particular moment, the bag's velocity is $10\\,\\text{m/s}$ and its instantaneous acceleration is $\\frac{49}{9}\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$ Substituting these values into the above, we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}294-k\\cdot {(10)}^{2} & =30\\cdot \\frac{49}{9} \\\\ 294-100k & =\\frac{1470}{9} \\\\ -100k & =-\\frac{390}{3} \\\\ k & =\\frac{392}{300} \\\\ k & =\\frac{98}{75}\\,\\text{kg/m}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the resultant force acting on the bag is\n\n$$\nF=294-\\frac{98}{75}{v}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWhen the bag reaches terminal velocity ${v}_{\\text{term}},$ the resultant force is zero:\n\n$$\n294-\\frac{98}{75}({v}_{\\text{term}})^{2}=0\\,⟹\\,{v}_{\\text{term}}=\\sqrt{225}=15\n$$\n\nTherefore, the terminal velocity of the bag is ${v}_{\\text{term}}=15\\,\\text{m/s}.$" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363938 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363939 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363938 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363938 1077 "field-1" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363938 1079 #edb/ref 17592186363939 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363938 1080 #uuid "1623b7dd-7f8d-5b07-98b4-f2ab281f01eb" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363939 1081 "15" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17