uch that $({\\alpha }_{i},{\\beta }_{i})\\ne (0,0).$ \n\nThe inhomogeneous BVP has at most one solution if and only if the associated homogeneous BVP has only the trivial solution $y≡0.$ Specifically:\n\n- If the homogeneous BVP has the unique solution $y≡0,$ then the inhomogeneous problem has a unique solution.\n\n- If the homogeneous BVP has (infinitely many) nontrivial solutions, then the inhomogeneous problem either has infinitely many solutions or no solution.\n\nLet ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ be a fundamental set of solutions to the corresponding homogeneous differential equation ${y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0.$ \n\nTo classify the solutions of the corresponding homogeneous BVP, we define the matrix $A$ as\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}\\,{\\alpha }_{1}{y}_{1}(a)+{\\beta }_{1}{y}_{1}^{′}(a) & {\\alpha }_{1}{y}_{2}(a)+{\\beta }_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}(a) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{2}{y}_{1}(b)+{\\beta }_{2}{y}_{1}^{′}(b) & {\\alpha }_{2}{y}_{2}(b)+{\\beta }_{2}{y}_{2}^{′}(b)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe homogeneous BVP has a unique, trivial solution $y≡0$ if and only if $det(A)\\ne 0.$ In other words, if the determinant equals zero, then the homogeneous BVP has *infinitely many solutions*.\n\nNotice that, in our case, we have Dirichlet boundary conditions. So, setting ${\\alpha }_{i}=1,{\\beta }_{i}=0,a=0,$ and $b=1,$ the matrix $A$ reduces to\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}(0) & {y}_{2}(0) \\\\ {y}_{1}(1) & {y}_{2}(1)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y={e}^{mx}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}-4m+3 & =0 \\\\ (m-1)(m-3) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have $m=1$ and $m=3,$ and so the fundamental solutions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{x},\\,{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{3x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix $A$ is\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ e & {e}^{3}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nand we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|det(A)| & =|1\\cdot {e}^{3}-1\\cdot e| \\\\  & =|{e}^{3}-e| \\\\  & =e({e}^{2}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(A)\\ne 0,$ the corresponding homogeneous BVP $\\text{has the unique solution }y≡0.$\n\nTherefore, since the corresponding homogeneous BVP has only the trivial solution $y≡0$, our inhomogeneous BVP $\\text{has a unique solution}.$" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363963 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363964 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363963 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363965 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363963 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363966 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363963 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045494 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363963 1077 "field-2" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363963 1079 #edb/ref 17592186363964 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363963 1080 #uuid "d98357ee-de9d-557d-ab2b-cab09749ec14" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363964 1081 "has the unique solution $y≡0$" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363964 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363964 1084 #uuid "640db27e-1b64-566e-a356-8324775e5d23" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363965 1081 "has infinitely many solutions" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363965 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363965 1084 #uuid "b3fac4a6-5923-54ba-a42e-5f1de8443227" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363966 1081 "has no solution" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363966 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363966 1084 #uuid "a011c14a-a54e-5e10-aac3-2c6710081ead" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363967 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363968 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363967 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363969 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363967 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363970 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363967 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363971 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363967 1076 #edb/ref 1759218604549