 fundamental solutions, and suppose we define the matrix $A$ as \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′}(0) & {y}_{2}^{′}(0) \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′}(\\pi ) & {y}_{2}^{′}(\\pi )\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFill in the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\n$\\,$ The **absolute value** of $det(A)$ is {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the corresponding homogeneous BVP {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, our inhomogeneous BVP {{field-3}}." 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363978 1072 #uuid "ff81f0a1-e38c-558c-9e6f-fc08059e49f0" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363978 1074 "Suppose we have the second-order inhomogeneous boundary value problem (BVP)\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(x)+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=f(x),\\,a\\le x\\le b\n$$\n\nwith the Robin (mixed) boundary conditions\n\n$$\n{\\alpha }_{1}y(a)+{\\beta }_{1}{y}^{′}(a)={\\gamma }_{1},\\,{\\alpha }_{2}y(b)+{\\beta }_{2}{y}^{′}(b)={\\gamma }_{2}\n$$\n\nwhere $P,Q,$ and $f$ are continuous on $[a,b],$ and ${\\alpha }_{i}$ and ${\\beta }_{i}$ are real constants such that $({\\alpha }_{i},{\\beta }_{i})\\ne (0,0).$ \n\nThe inhomogeneous BVP has at most one solution if and only if the associated homogeneous BVP has only the trivial solution $y≡0.$ Specifically:\n\n- If the homogeneous BVP has the unique solution $y≡0,$ then the inhomogeneous problem either has no solution or a unique solution.\n\n- If the homogeneous BVP has (infinitely many) nontrivial solutions, then the inhomogeneous problem either has infinitely many solutions or no solution.\n\nTo classify the solutions of the corresponding homogeneous BVP, we define the matrix $A$ as\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}\\,{\\alpha }_{1}{y}_{1}(a)+{\\beta }_{1}{y}_{1}^{′}(a) & {\\alpha }_{1}{y}_{2}(a)+{\\beta }_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}(a) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{2}{y}_{1}(b)+{\\beta }_{2}{y}_{1}^{′}(b) & {\\alpha }_{2}{y}_{2}(b)+{\\beta }_{2}{y}_{2}^{′}(b)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe homogeneous BVP has a unique, trivial solution $y≡0$ if and only if $det(A)\\ne 0.$ In other words, if the determinant equals zero, then the homogeneous BVP has *infinitely many solutions*.\n\nNotice that, in our case, we have Neumann boundary conditions. So, setting ${\\alpha }_{i}=0,{\\beta }_{i}=1,a=0,$ and $b=\\pi ,$ the matrix $A$ reduces to\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′}(0) & {y}_{2}^{′}(0) \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′}(\\pi ) & {y}_{2}^{′}(\\pi )\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y={e}^{mx}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}+49 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have $m=\\pm 7\\text{i}.$ So, the fundamental solutions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡7x,\\,{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡7x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}^{′}(x)=-7\\operatorname{sin}⁡7x,\\,{y}_{2}^{′}(x)=7\\operatorname{cos}⁡7x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix $A$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}-7\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7\\cdot 0) & 7\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7\\cdot 0) \\\\ -7\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7\\cdot \\pi ) & 7\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7\\cdot \\pi )\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 7 \\\\ 0 & -7\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|det(A)| & =|0\\cdot (-7)-7\\cdot 0| \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(A)=0,$ the corresponding homogeneous BVP $\\text{has infinitely many solutions}.$\n\nTherefore, since the corresponding homogeneous BVP has (infinitely many) nontrivial solutions, our inhomogeneous BVP $\\text{has infinitely many solutions or no solution}.$" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363979 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363980 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363979 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363981 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363979 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363982 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363979 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045494 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363979 1077 "field-2" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363979 1079 #edb/ref 17592186363981 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186363979 1080 #uuid 