a }_{i}$ and ${\\beta }_{i}$ are real constants such that $({\\alpha }_{i},{\\beta }_{i})\\ne (0,0).$ \n\nIf the associated homogeneous BVP has nontrivial solutions, then the inhomogeneous BVP has either no solution or infinitely many solutions. \n\nTo find the solution of an inhomogeneous boundary value problem (BVP):\n\n- First, we find the general solution ${y}_{h}(x)$ of the corresponding homogeneous equation.\n\n- Then, we find a particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ of the inhomogeneous equation.\n\n- Finally, we apply the boundary conditions to determine the arbitrary constants.\n\nHowever, in this particular case, we can integrate the equation directly. By doing so, we get the general solution\n\n$$\ny=-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+Ax+B.\n$$\n\n \n\nNote that the derivative ${y}^{′}$ is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-x+A.\n$$\n\nApplying the condition ${y}^{′}(-2)=2$ gives\n\n$$\n2+A=2\\,⇒\\,A=0.\n$$\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\ny=-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+B.\n$$\n\n \n\nApplying the condition ${y}^{′}(0)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-0+A & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is automatically satisfied for any $B.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the boundary value problem is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & =-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+B \\\\  & =(-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2})+B(1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\nf(x)=-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2},\\,g(x)=1.\n$$\n\n \n\n**Note**: The associated homogeneous BVP\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=0,\\,{y}^{′}(-2)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=0,\\,-2\\le x\\le 0\n$$\n\nhas infinitely many solutions. Therefore, the inhomogeneous BVP has either infinitely many solutions or no solution. Since we have found a solution that contains an arbitrary constant and satisfies both boundary conditions, the BVP has infinitely many solutions." 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186364068 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364069 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186364068 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364070 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186364068 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364071 13194139533446 17592186045585 tr