acteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda )=(\\lambda +2)(\\lambda -5)^{19},$ the rank of $(A-5I)^{5}$ equals $12,$ and the rank of $(A-5I)^{6}$ equals $9.$ If a basis of ${R}^{20}$ consists of linearly independent generalized eigenvectors of $A$, how many of these are generalized eigenvectors of rank $6$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =5?$" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186364440 1072 #uuid "d180b2be-eb75-50b7-9052-2821851f23f8" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186364440 1074 "For the given matrix $A$ with eigenvalue $\\lambda =5,$ a generalized eigenvector of rank $6$ is a vector $v$ such that \n\n$$\n(A-5I)^{6}v=0\\,\\text{and}\\,(A-5I)^{5}v\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nNotice that $A$ must be a $20\\times 20$ matrix.\n\n- Since the rank of $(A-5I)^{5}$ equals $12,$ then by the rank-nullity theorem, we have that\n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(A-5I)^{5}=20-12=8.\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions to $(A-5I)^{5}v=0$ form a subspace $\\text{Null}(A-5I)^{5}$ of dimension $8$ in ${R}^{20}.$\n\n- Since the rank of $(A-5I)^{6}$ equals $9,$  then by the rank-nullity theorem, we have that\n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(A-5I)^{6}=20-9=11.\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions to $(A-5I)^{6}v=0$ form a subspace $\\text{Null}(A-5I)^{6}$ of dimension $11$ in ${R}^{20}.$\n\nSince $\\text{Null}(A-2I)^{5}$ is a subspace of dimension $8$ in $\\text{Null}(A-5I)^{6}$, which is a subspace of dimension $11,$ our basis can contain only $3$ vectors that lie in $\\text{Null}(A-5I)^{6}$ but do not lie in $\\text{Null}(A-5I)^{5}.$\n\nTherefore, there will be \n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(A-5I)^{6}-\\text{nullity}(A-5I)^{5}=11-8=3\n$$\n\n generalized eigenvectors of rank $6$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =5.$" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186364441 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364442 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186364441 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364443 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186364441 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364444 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186364441 1075 #edb/ref