d "9021d181-6a6e-589c-8096-c7ad92921266" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186364654 1074 "Let $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ be the tank's length, width, and height, respectively. We wish to minimize the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=xy+2xz+2yz,\n$$\n\nsubject to the constraint \n\n$$\nxyz=256.\n$$\n\nNote that we also have the constraints $x>0,y>0,z>0.$\n\nLet $g(x,y,z)=xyz.$ Therefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z),\\,g(x,y,z)=256\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y,z),{f}_{y}(x,y,z),{f}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y,z),{g}_{y}(x,y,z),{g}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ \\\\ ⟨y+2z,x+2z,2x+2y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨yz,xz,xy⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y+2z=\\lambda yz \\\\ x+2z=\\lambda xz \\\\ 2x+2y=\\lambda xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the first, second, and third equations by $x,y,$ and $z$ respectively, we get the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x(y+2z)=\\lambda xyz \\\\ y(x+2z)=\\lambda xyz \\\\ z(2x+2y)=\\lambda xyz\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first and second equations above, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(y+2z) & =y(x+2z) \\\\ xy+2xz & =xy+2yz \\\\ 2xz & =2yz \\\\ 2z(x-y) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $z>0,$ we can conclude that $x=y.$\n\nSimilarly, from the second and third equations, we get that $x=y=2z.$ So, using the constraint equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}xyz & =256 \\\\ (2z)(2z)z & =256 \\\\ 4{z}^{3} & =256 \\\\ {z}^{3} & =64 \\\\ z & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is reached at the point $(8,8,4),$ and the minimum value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(8,8,4)=8(8)+2(8)(4)+2(8)(4)=192\\,{\\text{ft}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** It's easy to show that the obtained point is indeed the minimum (and not the maximum). To do that, we could compare the value of the function at this point with the value of the function at any other (arbitrary) point belonging to the domain. For example, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(16,16,1) & =(16)(16)+2(16)(1)+2(16)(1) \\\\  & =320 \\\\  & >f(8,8,4).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186364655 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364656 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186364655 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364657 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186364655 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364658 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186364655 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364659 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186364655 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364660 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186364655 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186364655 1077 "selection" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186364655 1079 #edb/ref 17592186364660 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186364655 1080 #uuid "6ae58a56-6faf-52c9-9561-c87d35603ea0" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186364656 1081 "243\\,{\\text{ft}}^{2}" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186364656 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186364656 1084 #uuid "f027768c-f196-5e3f-adf4-7554b2177b3b" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186364657 1081 "162\\,{\\text{ft}}^{2}" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186364657 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186364657 1084 #uuid "f96459e6-4979-5896-b4e6-40cb50e3fdd2" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186364658 1081 "128\\,{\\text{ft}}^{2}" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186364658 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186364658 1084 #uuid "5fe9f1a7-0904-5a92-b9f4-1e7ca2a2d4bb" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186364659 1081 "369\\,{\\text{ft}}^{2}" 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186364659 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533446 17592186045585 true] [17592186364659 1084 #uuid "dc20d337-eb61-592e-aeed-865846e45